{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QHkjvCrwqfA8AfZtjekaA1","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Implementation Issues Log\n     2→\n     3→This document records issues, deviations, and observations discovered during the implementation of the monorepo migration plan (`plan/merged/plan.md`).\n     4→\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→## Issue 1: Pre-existing typecheck failure unrelated to migration\n     8→\n     9→**Observed during**: Phase 6 verification (Step 6 - `bun run typecheck`).\n    10→\n    11→**Description**: Running `bun run typecheck` (which calls `tsc --noEmit`) fails with:\n    12→\n    13→```\n    14→error TS2688: Cannot find type definition file for 'bun-types'.\n    15→  The file is in the program because:\n    16→    Entry point of type library 'bun-types' specified in compilerOptions\n    17→```\n    18→\n    19→**Root cause**: The `bun-types` package is stored in `node_modules/.bun/bun-types@1.3.6/node_modules/bun-types/` rather than directly at `node_modules/bun-types/`. This means the TypeScript compiler (`tsc`) cannot find it via normal module resolution. The `@types/bun` package exists at `node_modules/@types/bun/index.d.ts` which is the newer recommended package, but `tsconfig.json` still references `\"types\": [\"bun-types\"]` (the older package name).\n    20→\n    21→**Confirmed pre-existing**: Verified by running `git stash` to revert all migration changes and re-running the typecheck — the identical failure occurs. This is not a regression introduced by the migration.\n    22→\n    23→**Impact on migration**: None. The plan's Phase 6.1 (`bun run tc`) step will surface this failure, but it should be noted as pre-existing in the plan. The plan's claim \"None of these should be affected by the migration\" is accurate.\n    24→\n    25→**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: Add a note in Section g (Testing Considerations) or Section f (Phase 6) that `bun run tc` has a pre-existing failure related to `bun-types` resolution that is unrelated to the monorepo migration. The future implementer should not be alarmed by this failure.\n    26→\n    27→---\n    28→\n    29→## Issue 2: Unit tests have 9 pre-existing failures unrelated to migration\n    30→\n    31→**Observed during**: Phase 6 verification (`bun run test:unit`).\n    32→\n    33→**Description**: 9 unit tests fail consistently with errors like:\n    34→\n    35→```\n    36→error: Codex binary not found in node_modules. Ensure @openai/codex-sdk is installed.\n    37→```\n    38→\n    39→and several `expect(result).not.toBeNull()` failures in what appear to be Codex locator tests.\n    40→\n    41→**Confirmed pre-existing**: Verified by running `git stash` and re-running the unit tests — the identical 9 failures occur. Total: 1512 pass, 4 skip, 9 fail.\n    42→\n    43→**Impact on migration**: None. These failures are unrelated to the workspace/docs changes.\n    44→\n    45→**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: Add a note in Section g (Testing Considerations) that the test suite has 9 pre-existing failures related to Codex binary resolution. The migration does not affect these tests.\n    46→\n    47→---\n    48→\n    49→## Issue 3: `bun install` regenerates a significantly larger bun.lock\n    50→\n    51→**Observed during**: Phase 1, Step 1.4 (`bun install`).\n    52→\n    53→**Description**: Running `bun install` after adding `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` to `package.json` regenerated `bun.lock` with approximately 1,071 additional lines (from the docs workspace dependencies: `next`, `nextra`, `react`, `posthog-js`, etc.). The final install completed successfully with \"1614 packages installed\" in ~8 seconds.\n    54→\n    55→**Peer dependency note**: The root `package.json` has `\"peerDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.9.2\" }` and the docs `package.json` has `\"devDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.0.0\" }`. Bun resolved this without warnings, choosing `typescript@5.9.3`. No peer dependency conflict was observed (the plan anticipated a possible warning; none materialized).\n    56→\n    57→**Impact**: The plan accurately described this step. The lockfile diff is large but expected.\n    58→\n    59→---\n    60→\n    61→## Issue 4: `schemas/hankweave.schema.json` changes after `bun install`\n    62→\n    63→**Observed during**: Post-`bun install` git diff.\n    64→\n    65→**Description**: Running `bun run generate-schemas` after `bun install` modified `schemas/hankweave.schema.json` with 4 lines changed. This is likely because the new `typescript@5.9.3` version resolved from the unified lockfile generated slightly different Zod-to-JSON output.\n    66→\n    67→**Impact**: Minor. The schema diff is cosmetic and captured in the migration commit. This is a one-time artifact of the dependency resolution. Future `generate-schemas` runs will be stable.\n    68→\n    69→---\n    70→\n    71→## Issue 5: The plan recommends `git checkout develop` in Phase 0 but the working directory is already the hankweave repo\n    72→\n    73→**Observed during**: Understanding the workspace layout during implementation.\n    74→\n    75→**Description**: The implementation is being applied directly to the `source/hankweave` directory (which is already the cloned hankweave git repo). Phase 0 in the plan describes creating a feature branch with `git checkout develop && git pull && git checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration`. In the actual workflow (as experienced by this agent), the work was done directly on `develop` without creating a feature branch.\n    76→\n    77→**Impact**: The migration changes are currently sitting as uncommitted modifications on `develop`. In a real production scenario, a feature branch should be created as Phase 0 describes. The plan is correct - the implementation deviated from it.\n    78→\n    79→**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: This is not a plan error, just a note about the actual execution context. The plan's Phase 0 remains correct for the real implementation scenario.\n    80→\n    81→---\n    82→\n    83→## Issue 6: `docs/bun.lock` was in nextra-site and needed to be deleted before `bun install`\n    84→\n    85→**Observed during**: Phase 1, Steps 1.1-1.4.\n    86→\n    87→**Description**: When copying `nextra-site/*` into `docs/`, the copy included `nextra-site/bun.lock`. The plan correctly identifies this in Step 1.3: \"Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`\". This was done with a plain `rm docs/bun.lock` before running `bun install`. Since the file had not yet been git-committed, `git rm` was not needed.\n    88→\n    89→**Impact**: None — the plan handled this correctly. Worth confirming the implementation followed the plan's precise sequence.\n    90→\n    91→---\n    92→\n    93→## Overall Assessment\n    94→\n    95→The implementation matched the plan closely. All 8 phases were executed successfully:\n    96→\n    97→1. `docs/` directory created with nextra-site contents (minus `bun.lock`)\n    98→2. `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` added to root `package.json`\n    99→3. `tsconfig.json` updated with `\"docs/**/*\"` in `exclude`\n   100→4. `generate-schemas.ts` path updated to use `docs/public/schemas/`\n   101→5. `sync-public.ts` updated: `STRIP_DIRS` + `delete pkg.workspaces`\n   102→6. `release.ts` updated: `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` + schema diff/revert/add\n   103→7. `test-sync-local.ts` updated: rsync `--exclude='docs'` + `shouldNotExist` list\n   104→8. `.gitignore` updated with docs build artifacts\n   105→9. `standard-operating-procedures.md` updated across all four sections\n   106→\n   107→Verification:\n   108→- `bun install`: success, 1614 packages, unified lockfile generated\n   109→- `bun run lint:fix`: clean (230 files, no fixes needed)\n   110→- `bun run generate-schemas`: success, schemas written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n   111→- `npm pack --dry-run`: `docs/` correctly excluded from tarball\n   112→- Unit tests: 1512 pass / 4 skip / 9 fail (9 failures pre-existing, not related to migration)\n   113→- Typecheck: pre-existing `bun-types` resolution failure (not related to migration)\n   114→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"774524ca-ef80-4770-b634-d06c9d7eeed4","uuid":"69d710da-c773-4088-aafa-3d97f3d2a223","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/implementations/current/implementation_issues.md","content":"# Implementation Issues Log\n\nThis document records issues, deviations, and observations discovered during the implementation of the monorepo migration plan (`plan/merged/plan.md`).\n\n---\n\n## Issue 1: Pre-existing typecheck failure unrelated to migration\n\n**Observed during**: Phase 6 verification (Step 6 - `bun run typecheck`).\n\n**Description**: Running `bun run typecheck` (which calls `tsc --noEmit`) fails with:\n\n```\nerror TS2688: Cannot find type definition file for 'bun-types'.\n  The file is in the program because:\n    Entry point of type library 'bun-types' specified in compilerOptions\n```\n\n**Root cause**: The `bun-types` package is stored in `node_modules/.bun/bun-types@1.3.6/node_modules/bun-types/` rather than directly at `node_modules/bun-types/`. This means the TypeScript compiler (`tsc`) cannot find it via normal module resolution. The `@types/bun` package exists at `node_modules/@types/bun/index.d.ts` which is the newer recommended package, but `tsconfig.json` still references `\"types\": [\"bun-types\"]` (the older package name).\n\n**Confirmed pre-existing**: Verified by running `git stash` to revert all migration changes and re-running the typecheck — the identical failure occurs. This is not a regression introduced by the migration.\n\n**Impact on migration**: None. The plan's Phase 6.1 (`bun run tc`) step will surface this failure, but it should be noted as pre-existing in the plan. The plan's claim \"None of these should be affected by the migration\" is accurate.\n\n**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: Add a note in Section g (Testing Considerations) or Section f (Phase 6) that `bun run tc` has a pre-existing failure related to `bun-types` resolution that is unrelated to the monorepo migration. The future implementer should not be alarmed by this failure.\n\n---\n\n## Issue 2: Unit tests have 9 pre-existing failures unrelated to migration\n\n**Observed during**: Phase 6 verification (`bun run test:unit`).\n\n**Description**: 9 unit tests fail consistently with errors like:\n\n```\nerror: Codex binary not found in node_modules. Ensure @openai/codex-sdk is installed.\n```\n\nand several `expect(result).not.toBeNull()` failures in what appear to be Codex locator tests.\n\n**Confirmed pre-existing**: Verified by running `git stash` and re-running the unit tests — the identical 9 failures occur. Total: 1512 pass, 4 skip, 9 fail.\n\n**Impact on migration**: None. These failures are unrelated to the workspace/docs changes.\n\n**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: Add a note in Section g (Testing Considerations) that the test suite has 9 pre-existing failures related to Codex binary resolution. The migration does not affect these tests.\n\n---\n\n## Issue 3: `bun install` regenerates a significantly larger bun.lock\n\n**Observed during**: Phase 1, Step 1.4 (`bun install`).\n\n**Description**: Running `bun install` after adding `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` to `package.json` regenerated `bun.lock` with approximately 1,071 additional lines (from the docs workspace dependencies: `next`, `nextra`, `react`, `posthog-js`, etc.). The final install completed successfully with \"1614 packages installed\" in ~8 seconds.\n\n**Peer dependency note**: The root `package.json` has `\"peerDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.9.2\" }` and the docs `package.json` has `\"devDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.0.0\" }`. Bun resolved this without warnings, choosing `typescript@5.9.3`. No peer dependency conflict was observed (the plan anticipated a possible warning; none materialized).\n\n**Impact**: The plan accurately described this step. The lockfile diff is large but expected.\n\n---\n\n## Issue 4: `schemas/hankweave.schema.json` changes after `bun install`\n\n**Observed during**: Post-`bun install` git diff.\n\n**Description**: Running `bun run generate-schemas` after `bun install` modified `schemas/hankweave.schema.json` with 4 lines changed. This is likely because the new `typescript@5.9.3` version resolved from the unified lockfile generated slightly different Zod-to-JSON output.\n\n**Impact**: Minor. The schema diff is cosmetic and captured in the migration commit. This is a one-time artifact of the dependency resolution. Future `generate-schemas` runs will be stable.\n\n---\n\n## Issue 5: The plan recommends `git checkout develop` in Phase 0 but the working directory is already the hankweave repo\n\n**Observed during**: Understanding the workspace layout during implementation.\n\n**Description**: The implementation is being applied directly to the `source/hankweave` directory (which is already the cloned hankweave git repo). Phase 0 in the plan describes creating a feature branch with `git checkout develop && git pull && git checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration`. In the actual workflow (as experienced by this agent), the work was done directly on `develop` without creating a feature branch.\n\n**Impact**: The migration changes are currently sitting as uncommitted modifications on `develop`. In a real production scenario, a feature branch should be created as Phase 0 describes. The plan is correct - the implementation deviated from it.\n\n**Recommended follow-up for plan update**: This is not a plan error, just a note about the actual execution context. The plan's Phase 0 remains correct for the real implementation scenario.\n\n---\n\n## Issue 6: `docs/bun.lock` was in nextra-site and needed to be deleted before `bun install`\n\n**Observed during**: Phase 1, Steps 1.1-1.4.\n\n**Description**: When copying `nextra-site/*` into `docs/`, the copy included `nextra-site/bun.lock`. The plan correctly identifies this in Step 1.3: \"Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`\". This was done with a plain `rm docs/bun.lock` before running `bun install`. Since the file had not yet been git-committed, `git rm` was not needed.\n\n**Impact**: None — the plan handled this correctly. Worth confirming the implementation followed the plan's precise sequence.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe implementation matched the plan closely. All 8 phases were executed successfully:\n\n1. `docs/` directory created with nextra-site contents (minus `bun.lock`)\n2. `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` added to root `package.json`\n3. `tsconfig.json` updated with `\"docs/**/*\"` in `exclude`\n4. `generate-schemas.ts` path updated to use `docs/public/schemas/`\n5. `sync-public.ts` updated: `STRIP_DIRS` + `delete pkg.workspaces`\n6. `release.ts` updated: `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` + schema diff/revert/add\n7. `test-sync-local.ts` updated: rsync `--exclude='docs'` + `shouldNotExist` list\n8. `.gitignore` updated with docs build artifacts\n9. `standard-operating-procedures.md` updated across all four sections\n\nVerification:\n- `bun install`: success, 1614 packages, unified lockfile generated\n- `bun run lint:fix`: clean (230 files, no fixes needed)\n- `bun run generate-schemas`: success, schemas written to both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`\n- `npm pack --dry-run`: `docs/` correctly excluded from tarball\n- Unit tests: 1512 pass / 4 skip / 9 fail (9 failures pre-existing, not related to migration)\n- Typecheck: pre-existing `bun-types` resolution failure (not related to migration)\n","numLines":114,"startLine":1,"totalLines":114}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:30:44.989Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UVVjSvu6FEVnm4mJfV5N4p","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Plan: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n     2→\n     3→## a. The Main Task\n     4→\n     5→Consolidate the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` (runtime) and `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` (documentation site) repositories into a single monorepo using Bun workspaces. The runtime package stays at the repository root. The docs site moves in as a workspace at `docs/`, flattened from its current `nextra-site/` subdirectory. The existing CI/CD pipelines, release process, public sync workflow, and Vercel docs deployment continue to function with targeted, minimal changes.\n     6→\n     7→---\n     8→\n     9→## b. Information Available\n    10→\n    11→### Sources that informed this plan\n    12→\n    13→1. **Task specification** -- `plan/task.md` (task description, open questions, research on Bun workspaces, Turborepo, and git subtree merges).\n    14→2. **Opus plan and addendums** -- `plan/rough-workspace/opus/plan.md`, `addendum-docs.md`, `addendum-tests.md`, `log.md`. Detailed, phase-by-phase plan with exact file diffs, SOP drafts, and edge case analysis.\n    15→3. **Codex plan and addendums** -- `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`, `addendum-1.md` (tests/CI), `addendum-2.md` (docs/deployment). Shorter plan with a notable catch on the `transformPackageJson()` gap for stripping `\"workspaces\"` from the public `package.json`.\n    16→4. **Full source code review** -- Every config, script, workflow, and representative server/test file from both `source/hankweave/` and `source/hankweave-docs/`.\n    17→\n    18→### What I could see\n    19→\n    20→- Full directory structure and file contents of both repos.\n    21→- All CI workflows (ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml), composite actions (setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs), and scripts (build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, test-sync-local.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts).\n    22→- Both `package.json` files, both `tsconfig.json` files, `biome.json`, `bunfig.toml`.\n    23→- The full docs Nextra/Next.js setup including `vercel.json`, `next.config.mjs`, `app/`, `content/`, `components/`, `public/`, `styles/`.\n    24→- The SOP document (`standard-operating-procedures.md`).\n    25→- The `.gitignore` files from both repos.\n    26→\n    27→### What I could NOT see\n    28→\n    29→- The actual GitHub repos (branch protection rules, secrets, default branches, collaborator list).\n    30→- The Vercel project dashboard settings (current root directory config, branch deployment rules).\n    31→- The `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) repo contents -- only references to it in scripts and workflows.\n    32→- Linear project / ticket context (no ticket ID provided).\n    33→- Whether there are other repos or tools beyond what's in the source snapshot that the task spec's \"related packages/tools\" refers to.\n    34→\n    35→### Assumptions\n    36→\n    37→1. The `hankweave-docs` repo does not have significant git history worth preserving. The task spec describes history preservation as \"nice to have.\" The default approach is a simple file copy with a descriptive commit. The git subtree method is documented in Section i as an optional escape hatch only — not the recommended path.\n    38→2. The public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`) continues to exist as a separate repo. This migration consolidates the two private development repos only.\n    39→3. The docs site continues to deploy to Vercel, now from the monorepo.\n    40→4. No Turborepo or other task orchestration is needed for two packages.\n    41→5. The `hankweave-docs/` outer wrapper (README, `notes/`, `plan/`) is development scaffolding that does not need to be carried into the monorepo.\n    42→\n    43→---\n    44→\n    45→## c. Sources and Related Things\n    46→\n    47→### Original task\n    48→\n    49→- Task description: `plan/task.md`\n    50→- No Linear ticket ID provided.\n    51→\n    52→### Code references\n    53→\n    54→| File | Line(s) | Relevance |\n    55→|------|---------|-----------|\n    56→| `source/hankweave/package.json` | Full | Root package.json -- needs `workspaces` field added |\n    57→| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | Full | Docs package.json -- moves to `docs/package.json` |\n    58→| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 24-32 | The only cross-repo code dependency; `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path |\n    59→| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 76 | `docsAvailable` check -- will become permanently true |\n    60→| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 28 | `STRIP_DIRS` -- needs `\"docs\"` added |\n    61→| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 173-196 | `transformPackageJson()` -- needs to strip `\"workspaces\"` |\n    62→| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 54-60 | `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` -- needs `\"docs/\"` added |\n    63→| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 436 | Schema revert in validation -- needs to also revert `docs/public/schemas/` |\n    64→| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 510 | Release commit `git add` -- needs to also add `docs/public/schemas/` |\n    65→| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 80 | `--use-head` rsync -- needs `--exclude='docs'` added |\n    66→| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 160-175 | `shouldNotExist` verification list -- needs `\"docs\"` added |\n    67→| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Full | CI workflow -- minimal changes needed |\n    68→| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | Full | Release workflow -- minimal changes |\n    69→| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Full | Sync workflow -- no code changes needed |\n    70→| `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml` | 34 | `bun install --frozen-lockfile` -- works with workspaces |\n    71→| `source/hankweave/biome.json` | 4-11 | Linting scope whitelist -- only covers runtime files |\n    72→| `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Full | Bun config -- no changes needed |\n    73→| `source/hankweave/tsconfig.json` | Full | Runtime TS config -- needs `\"docs/**/*\"` added to `exclude` |\n    74→| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json` | Full | Docs TS config -- stays as-is at new location |\n    75→| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Full | Vercel config -- stays in docs workspace |\n    76→| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/next.config.mjs` | Full | Next.js config -- no changes |\n    77→| `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | 10-30, 31-40, 337-354 | Repo structure, strip list docs, key files |\n    78→| `source/hankweave/.gitignore` | Full | May need additions for docs build artifacts |\n    79→| `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts` | 402-410 | `getMetadata()` reads `../package.json` -- structural assumption |\n    80→\n    81→### Research sources\n    82→\n    83→- [Bun Workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) -- native workspace support via `\"workspaces\"` field in `package.json`\n    84→- [Bun Catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs) -- optional shared dependency versioning (not needed initially)\n    85→- [Git subtree merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges) -- for preserving docs repo history\n    86→\n    87→---\n    88→\n    89→## d. Conventions and Patterns\n    90→\n    91→### Observed code conventions\n    92→\n    93→1. **Scripts are TypeScript.** All build/release/sync scripts are `.ts` files run with `bun`.\n    94→2. **Relative paths from `import.meta.dir`.** Scripts use `path.join(import.meta.dir, \"..\")` to resolve project root.\n    95→3. **`Bun.spawn` for shell commands.** Scripts use `Bun.spawn([\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd])`. Note: each script defines its own `exec()` helper with different signatures. `sync-public.ts` has `exec(cmd, { cwd?, silent? })`, while `release.ts` has `exec(command, { silent?, allowFailure? })`. These are not shared. The implementing agent should not assume one script's `exec()` options exist in the other.\n    96→4. **Two-space indent, 100-char line width.** Enforced by Biome.\n    97→5. **LF line endings.** Enforced by `.gitattributes`.\n    98→6. **No shared state between packages.** Docs don't import from runtime; only schema JSON files are copied by a script.\n    99→7. **Strip lists are explicit.** Both `sync-public.ts` and `release.ts` maintain explicit lists of files/dirs to remove. Adding a new internal directory means adding it to both.\n   100→8. **Runtime is strict ESM TypeScript** with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `import \"./utils.js\"`).\n   101→9. **`server/utils.ts` reads `../package.json`** for version metadata. This assumes `server/` is one level below the package root.\n   102→\n   103→### Patterns to follow\n   104→\n   105→- Add `\"docs\"` to existing strip lists rather than creating new mechanisms.\n   106→- Keep the root `package.json` as the runtime's `package.json` (add only the `\"workspaces\"` field).\n   107→- Don't introduce Turborepo or other tooling -- Bun workspaces is sufficient for two packages.\n   108→- Keep separate `tsconfig.json` per workspace (they have incompatible settings: runtime targets ES2022 with strict mode and bun-types; docs targets ES2020 with strict disabled and Next.js plugin).\n   109→- Don't introduce `workspace:*` dependencies between docs and runtime. The schema copy is a script-level file operation, not a package dependency.\n   110→\n   111→### Patterns to avoid\n   112→\n   113→- Don't move the runtime into a subdirectory. This would break dozens of paths across scripts, CI workflows, and `server/utils.ts` metadata resolution.\n   114→- Don't create a shared `tsconfig.base.json`. The two configs are too different.\n   115→- Don't add workspace filter flags to CI scripts. For two packages, let everything install and run from root.\n   116→- Don't extend `biome.json` to cover `docs/`. The docs site uses React/JSX conventions and Next.js tooling that are not part of the runtime's Biome setup.\n   117→\n   118→---\n   119→\n   120→## e. Decision Points and Judgement Calls\n   121→\n   122→### Decision 1: Keep the runtime at the repo root\n   123→\n   124→**Chosen**: The runtime stays at root. The docs site moves into `docs/`.\n   125→\n   126→**Reasoning**: The runtime has 8 scripts, 3 CI workflows, 3 composite actions, build/release/sync pipelines, and extensive relative path usage (scripts use `import.meta.dir` + `\"..\"`, `server/utils.ts` reads `../package.json`, build scripts use `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point). Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing every one of these. The docs site has no CI of its own, no complex scripts, and a single `package.json`.\n   127→\n   128→**Trade-off**: The root `package.json` serves dual purpose as both workspace root and runtime package. This is a recognized Bun workspace pattern -- the root package is an \"implicit workspace member\" by default.\n   129→\n   130→**Alternative considered**: Both packages in `packages/` subdirectories. This is the conventional monorepo layout but would be a massive path-rewrite refactor (build scripts, CI workflows, composite actions, `import.meta.dir` references, `server/utils.ts` metadata resolution). Not justified for two packages.\n   131→\n   132→### Decision 2: Flatten the docs into `docs/`\n   133→\n   134→**Chosen**: Move `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/*` contents directly into `docs/` (not `docs/nextra-site/` or `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`).\n   135→\n   136→**Reasoning**: The `nextra-site/` wrapper exists because the docs repo has a root with README, `notes/`, and `plan/` directories. In the monorepo, these don't need a separate layer. Flattening gives a clean workspace path (`\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`), shorter references everywhere, and follows common conventions where `docs/` is a well-understood directory name.\n   137→\n   138→**Alternative considered**: Keeping `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` intact (the Codex recommendation). This preserves the original structure verbatim but results in the awkward path `\"workspaces\": [\"hankweave-docs/nextra-site\"]` and an unnecessary depth of nesting. Every reference to the docs workspace becomes longer.\n   139→\n   140→**What happens to `hankweave-docs/` non-nextra files**: The `README.md` at docs repo root is optional and can be included as `docs/README.md` or discarded. The `notes/` and `plan/` directories are historical development artifacts -- discard them. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/` (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration-legacy/`).\n   141→\n   142→### Decision 3: No `workspace:*` dependencies\n   143→\n   144→**Chosen**: No workspace dependency between docs and runtime.\n   145→\n   146→**Reasoning**: The docs site doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only data flow is schema JSON files copied by `generate-schemas.ts`. This is a script-level file copy, not a package dependency. Adding `workspace:*` would incorrectly suggest the docs depend on the runtime as a library.\n   147→\n   148→**When to revisit**: If the docs ever need to import TypeScript types or utilities from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference), add `workspace:*` at that point.\n   149→\n   150→### Decision 4: Convert the existing hankweave repo, don't create new\n   151→\n   152→**Chosen**: Modify `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` in-place to become the monorepo.\n   153→\n   154→**Reasoning**: All CI workflows, GitHub secrets, branch protection rules, Linear integration, and team permissions are already configured on this repo. Creating a new repo would require reconfiguring everything. The existing git history stays intact. The docs files are brought in via a merge commit (or `git subtree add` if history preservation is desired).\n   155→\n   156→### Decision 5: No Turborepo\n   157→\n   158→**Chosen**: Use Bun workspaces alone.\n   159→\n   160→**Reasoning**: Two packages don't need task orchestration or caching. The runtime builds and tests independently. The docs site builds independently on Vercel. There's no dependency chain requiring `^build` semantics. Adding Turborepo would increase complexity with no clear benefit.\n   161→\n   162→**When to revisit**: If more packages are added (e.g., a shared types package, a CLI package), or if CI times become problematic and caching would help.\n   163→\n   164→### Decision 6: Single lockfile at root\n   165→\n   166→**Chosen**: One `bun.lock` at the repo root, replacing both existing lockfiles.\n   167→\n   168→**Reasoning**: Bun workspaces uses a single root lockfile. Both workspace packages' dependencies are resolved and locked together. The first `bun install` after migration regenerates this. The docs-side `bun.lock` (from `nextra-site/`) is deleted.\n   169→\n   170→### Decision 7: Strip `\"workspaces\"` from public `package.json`\n   171→\n   172→**Chosen**: Modify `transformPackageJson()` in `sync-public.ts` to delete `pkg.workspaces` before writing the public `package.json`.\n   173→\n   174→**Reasoning**: The public mirror will not contain the `docs/` directory (it's stripped by `STRIP_DIRS`). If the public `package.json` ships with `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`, it references a directory that doesn't exist, which is confusing and could cause unexpected behavior when someone runs `bun install` or `npm install` on the public repo. This was identified by the Codex plan and confirmed by code review -- the current `transformPackageJson()` function (at `sync-public.ts:173-196`) changes `name`, `publishConfig`, `homepage`, and strips some scripts, but does not touch `workspaces`.\n   175→\n   176→### Over/under engineering line\n   177→\n   178→- **Under (acceptable)**: Not adding `paths:` filtering to CI triggers. Docs changes trigger runtime tests, but CI runs are fast enough that this isn't worth the complexity for two packages.\n   179→- **Under (acceptable)**: Not using Bun catalogs for shared TypeScript version. The projects only share TypeScript as a dev dependency and can version independently.\n   180→- **Over (avoid)**: Adding Turborepo, shared tsconfig base, workspace link scripts, or monorepo-specific CI orchestration tooling.\n   181→- **Right level**: Adding `\"docs\"` to strip lists, updating one path in `generate-schemas.ts`, updating the SOP, stripping `\"workspaces\"` from public package.json. Minimal changes that make the monorepo work correctly.\n   182→\n   183→---\n   184→\n   185→## f. The Actual Implementation Plan\n   186→\n   187→### Phase 0: Preparation\n   188→\n   189→**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n   190→```bash\n   191→git checkout develop\n   192→git pull origin develop\n   193→git checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration\n   194→```\n   195→\n   196→**Step 0.2: Verify clean state**\n   197→```bash\n   198→bun run test:unit\n   199→bun run lint\n   200→bun run tc\n   201→git status  # must be clean\n   202→```\n   203→\n   204→### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n   205→\n   206→**Step 1.1: Create the `docs/` directory**\n   207→\n   208→Before copying, verify that no `docs/` directory already exists in the repo (e.g., from a prior attempt). If it does, remove it first or abort and investigate. The source snapshot confirms `docs/` does not currently exist in `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n   209→\n   210→Copy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/` at the runtime repo root. The files to bring in:\n   211→\n   212→```\n   213→app/\n   214→components/\n   215→content/\n   216→public/\n   217→styles/\n   218→bun.lock           (will be removed in step 1.3)\n   219→mdx-components.tsx\n   220→next-env.d.ts\n   221→next.config.mjs\n   222→package.json\n   223→tsconfig.json\n   224→vercel.json\n   225→```\n   226→\n   227→Do NOT copy the outer `hankweave-docs/` wrapper (`notes/`, `plan/`). Optionally include `hankweave-docs/README.md` as `docs/README.md` for internal reference.\n   228→\n   229→Note: the `hankweave-docs` repo's `.gitignore` lives at the outer wrapper level (`hankweave-docs/.gitignore`), not inside `nextra-site/`. Since we are copying `nextra-site/*` into `docs/`, no `.gitignore` is carried over. The root `.gitignore` already covers the relevant patterns (see Phase 4).\n   230→\n   231→If git history preservation is desired, use `git subtree add`:\n   232→```bash\n   233→git remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\n   234→git merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\n   235→git read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\n   236→git commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n   237→```\n   238→Then restructure: move `docs/nextra-site/*` up to `docs/` and remove `docs/notes/`, `docs/plan/`, `docs/README.md`, `docs/.gitignore`.\n   239→\n   240→Alternatively, a simple file copy with a descriptive commit is simpler and the docs repo history remains available in the original repo if ever needed.\n   241→\n   242→**Step 1.2: Add workspace configuration to root `package.json`**\n   243→\n   244→Add the `\"workspaces\"` field to `source/hankweave/package.json`. Place it after `\"engines\"` and before `\"scripts\"`, adjacent to other structural metadata fields. The current field order is: `name`, `version`, `description`, `author`, `keywords`, `homepage`, `type`, `publishConfig`, `bin`, `main`, `files`, `engines`, `scripts`, `devDependencies`, `peerDependencies`, `dependencies`. The result:\n   245→\n   246→```json\n   247→{\n   248→  \"name\": \"@southbridgeai/hankweave\",\n   249→  \"version\": \"0.5.6\",\n   250→  ...\n   251→  \"engines\": { \"node\": \">=20.0.0\" },\n   252→  \"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],\n   253→  \"scripts\": { ... },\n   254→  ...\n   255→}\n   256→```\n   257→\n   258→The actual edit is a single line addition: insert `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],` between the closing `}` of `\"engines\"` and the opening `\"scripts\"` key.\n   259→\n   260→The root package is itself an implicit workspace member in Bun workspaces, so the runtime's own dependencies, scripts, and `\"files\"` field continue to work unchanged.\n   261→\n   262→**Step 1.3: Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`**\n   263→\n   264→Remove `docs/bun.lock` with a plain filesystem `rm docs/bun.lock`. This file was just copied in Step 1.1 and has not been committed to git yet, so `git rm` is not needed. The root lockfile will manage all dependencies after `bun install` in Step 1.4.\n   265→\n   266→**Step 1.4: Run `bun install`**\n   267→\n   268→From the repo root:\n   269→```bash\n   270→bun install\n   271→```\n   272→\n   273→This resolves dependencies for both workspaces, generates a new unified `bun.lock` at root, and creates a single hoisted `node_modules/` tree. The docs dependencies (`next`, `nextra`, `react`, `posthog-js`, etc.) will be added to the root lockfile but should not conflict with the runtime's dependencies — the two packages have almost entirely different dependency trees.\n   274→\n   275→Check the `bun install` output for warnings. One potential friction point: the root `package.json` has `\"peerDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.9.2\" }` while the docs `package.json` has `\"devDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.0.0\" }`. Bun may emit a peer dependency mismatch warning. This is cosmetic — it does not affect the build — but if it does, the docs `typescript` range could be narrowed to `\"^5.9.2\"` to match.\n   276→\n   277→**Step 1.5: Verify docs build locally**\n   278→\n   279→```bash\n   280→cd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n   281→```\n   282→\n   283→Verify the Next.js build succeeds. The `postbuild` step (Pagefind search indexing) should also run. If the build fails, the most likely cause is module resolution issues due to dependency hoisting. Bun workspaces hoist dependencies to the root `node_modules/`. The docs' dependencies (`next`, `nextra`, `react`, etc.) will land in the root `node_modules/`, not `docs/node_modules/`. ESM imports from `docs/next.config.mjs` (e.g., `import nextra from 'nextra'`) should resolve via Node's upward traversal, but if they don't, the fallback is to run `bun install` explicitly from `docs/` to create a local `node_modules/` with symlinks.\n   284→\n   285→### Phase 2: Update cross-repo references\n   286→\n   287→**Step 2.1: Exclude `docs/` from root `tsconfig.json`**\n   288→\n   289→The root `tsconfig.json` has `\"include\": [\"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\"]` which will pick up every TypeScript and TSX file in the repository. After migration, this glob matches `docs/**/*.ts` and `docs/**/*.tsx` — files that are incompatible with the runtime's compiler options. The docs workspace uses `\"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\"`, `\"module\": \"esnext\"`, `\"jsx\": \"preserve\"`, and `\"strict\": false`, while the runtime uses `\"moduleResolution\": \"node\"`, `\"module\": \"ES2022\"`, no JSX, and `\"strict\": true`. Running `bun run tc` (which invokes `tsc --noEmit` with the root tsconfig) will fail on docs files unless they are excluded.\n   290→\n   291→Add `\"docs/**/*\"` to the `exclude` array in the root `tsconfig.json`:\n   292→\n   293→```json\n   294→\"exclude\": [\n   295→  \"node_modules\",\n   296→  \".logs/**/*\",\n   297→  \"intermediates/**/*\",\n   298→  \"learning/**/*\",\n   299→  \"tests/test-area/**/*\",\n   300→  \"docs/**/*\"\n   301→]\n   302→```\n   303→\n   304→This is a required change. Without it, the migration will break `bun run tc`, which is run in CI and during release validation.\n   305→\n   306→**Step 2.2: Update `scripts/generate-schemas.ts`**\n   307→\n   308→Change the `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (lines 24-32) from:\n   309→```typescript\n   310→const DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n   311→  import.meta.dir,\n   312→  \"..\",\n   313→  \"..\",\n   314→  \"hankweave-docs\",\n   315→  \"nextra-site\",\n   316→  \"public\",\n   317→  \"schemas\",\n   318→);\n   319→```\n   320→\n   321→To:\n   322→```typescript\n   323→const DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n   324→  import.meta.dir,\n   325→  \"..\",\n   326→  \"docs\",\n   327→  \"public\",\n   328→  \"schemas\",\n   329→);\n   330→```\n   331→\n   332→The `docsAvailable` check at line 76 (`fs.existsSync(path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR))`) will now always evaluate to true since `docs/public/` is always present in the monorepo. Note the precision here: `path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR)` resolves to `docs/public/` (the parent of `schemas/`), not `docs/` itself. If `docs/public/` exists but `docs/public/schemas/` does not, the `mkdirSync` at line 78 creates it — that is correct behavior.\n   333→\n   334→Keep the check for defensive safety — it doesn't hurt, and it means the script still works correctly if someone runs it from an unexpected working directory (e.g., sparse checkout). Update the comment at line 75 to reflect the new context. The existing comment says \"optional - only used if hosting schemas on hankweave.dev\" — it should say something like \"Always available in monorepo; guard checks docs/public/ existence and is kept for safety in edge cases (sparse checkout, unusual working directory).\" The silent-skip behavior means a misconfiguration would produce no error and simply not write schemas to docs — worth being aware of, though unlikely in normal operation.\n   335→\n   336→**Step 2.3: Run schema generation to verify**\n   337→\n   338→```bash\n   339→bun run generate-schemas\n   340→ls schemas/hank.schema.json\n   341→ls docs/public/schemas/hank.schema.json\n   342→```\n   343→\n   344→Verify schemas are written to both `schemas/` (runtime's local copy for npm) and `docs/public/schemas/` (docs site's static files).\n   345→\n   346→### Phase 3: Update strip lists for public sync\n   347→\n   348→This phase has five changes across three scripts, plus an update to the `transformPackageJson()` function. All serve the same purpose: ensure the docs workspace doesn't leak into the public mirror and that schema handling covers both output directories.\n   349→\n   350→**Step 3.1: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` `STRIP_DIRS`**\n   351→\n   352→At line 28, add `\"docs\"`:\n   353→```typescript\n   354→const STRIP_DIRS = [\"intermediates\", \"external-docs\", \"public-release-files\", \".github/actions\", \"docs\"];\n   355→```\n   356→\n   357→This single constant change covers two consumers in the script: `prepareSnapshot()` (line 214, which builds rsync excludes with trailing slashes from `STRIP_DIRS`) and `initialSync()` (lines 291–302, which iterates `STRIP_DIRS` to `rm -rf` each directory from the public clone). `snapshotSync()` does not reference `STRIP_DIRS` directly — it copies the pre-built snapshot directory, which was already stripped by `prepareSnapshot()`. The implementing agent does not need to touch any of these functions — they all operate from the shared `STRIP_DIRS` constant or its output.\n   358→\n   359→**Step 3.2: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` `transformPackageJson()`**\n   360→\n   361→At line 173-196 in the `transformPackageJson()` function, add a line to delete the `\"workspaces\"` field from the public `package.json`:\n   362→\n   363→```typescript\n   364→async function transformPackageJson(packagePath: string): Promise<void> {\n   365→  const content = await readFile(packagePath, \"utf-8\");\n   366→  const pkg = JSON.parse(content);\n   367→\n   368→  pkg.name = \"hankweave\";\n   369→  pkg.publishConfig = { access: \"public\" };\n   370→  pkg.homepage = \"https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime\";\n   371→\n   372→  // Remove monorepo-only fields that don't apply to the public repo\n   373→  delete pkg.workspaces;\n   374→\n   375→  // Remove scripts that reference stripped internal files\n   376→  if (pkg.scripts) {\n   377→    for (const script of STRIP_SCRIPTS) {\n   378→      delete pkg.scripts[script];\n   379→    }\n   380→  }\n   381→\n   382→  await writeFile(packagePath, JSON.stringify(pkg, null, 2) + \"\\n\");\n   383→  // ... logging\n   384→}\n   385→```\n   386→\n   387→The code snippet above shows the full function for context, but the actual edit is minimal: insert `delete pkg.workspaces;` (with a comment) on a new line between line 179 (`pkg.homepage = ...`) and line 181 (`// Remove scripts...`). There is a blank line 180 between these — the insert goes there. Do not rewrite the entire function — the existing `console.log` lines at 189–195 are unchanged.\n   388→\n   389→This is critical. Without it, the public `package.json` would contain `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` pointing to a directory that doesn't exist in the public mirror.\n   390→\n   391→**Step 3.3: Update `scripts/release.ts` `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`**\n   392→\n   393→At line 54, add `\"docs/\"`:\n   394→```typescript\n   395→const INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP = [\n   396→  \"intermediates/\",\n   397→  \"external-docs/\",\n   398→  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n   399→  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n   400→  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n   401→  \"docs/\",\n   402→];\n   403→```\n   404→\n   405→This ensures the docs workspace is stripped from `release/alpha` during the release process (the `develop -> release/alpha` merge step). An important subtlety: the release script already has logic (at lines 366-385) to auto-resolve modify/delete merge conflicts on files in the `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` list. Once `\"docs/\"` is added, any docs changes on `develop` that conflict with the stripped `release/alpha` branch will be handled automatically. This is correct behavior and requires no additional code.\n   406→\n   407→Note on the `startsWith` check: the conflict auto-resolution logic at `release.ts:380` checks `f.startsWith(s)` against entries in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`. Git reports file paths relative to repo root, e.g., `docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`. Since the strip entry is `\"docs/\"`, the `startsWith` match evaluates correctly: `\"docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx\".startsWith(\"docs/\")` is `true`. This has been verified against the code.\n   408→\n   409→**Step 3.4: Update `scripts/release.ts` schema handling for docs**\n   410→\n   411→After migration, `generate-schemas` writes schemas to both `schemas/` (runtime) and `docs/public/schemas/` (docs site). The release script needs three fixes to account for this:\n   412→\n   413→1. **Validation phase schema diff check** (line 427): The `git diff --name-only schemas/` check that warns about schema changes only covers `schemas/`. Extend it to also check `docs/public/schemas/`:\n   414→\n   415→```typescript\n   416→const schemaChanges = await exec(\"git diff --name-only schemas/ docs/public/schemas/\", {\n   417→  silent: true,\n   418→});\n   419→```\n   420→\n   421→Without this, the validation phase would silently miss changes to docs-side schemas. This is minor (both directories are generated from the same source), but including the check keeps the warning accurate.\n   422→\n   423→2. **Validation phase revert** (around line 436): The `git checkout` that reverts test schema generation currently only covers `schemas/`. Extend it to also revert `docs/public/schemas/`:\n   424→\n   425→```typescript\n   426→await exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ docs/public/schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n   427→  silent: true,\n   428→  allowFailure: true,\n   429→});\n   430→```\n   431→\n   432→The implementing agent should copy the exact structure of the existing line at line 436 and extend the path string only — do not refactor the error handling pattern. The existing code uses a belt-and-suspenders approach: shell-level `2>/dev/null || true` suppresses the error output and exit code, AND `allowFailure: true` prevents the `exec()` helper from throwing. Both are redundant with each other but both must be maintained because this is the existing pattern in the file. If `docs/public/schemas/` does not yet exist in the git index (e.g., schemas have never been generated and committed), `git checkout -- docs/public/schemas/` will fail, and both error suppression levels handle this gracefully.\n   433→\n   434→Without this fix, the validation phase leaves `docs/public/schemas/` in a modified-but-unstaged state, which is messy and could cause the `git status --porcelain` check (at `release.ts:210`) in subsequent release runs to detect uncommitted changes.\n   435→\n   436→3. **Execute phase git add** (line 510): The release commit stages `package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/` but not `docs/public/schemas/`. After migration, schema generation writes to both directories, and the release commit should include both:\n   437→\n   438→```typescript\n   439→await exec(\"git add package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/ docs/public/schemas/\");\n   440→```\n   441→\n   442→Without this, the docs-side schemas would drift out of sync with the runtime schemas after each release — the runtime `schemas/` directory would be updated in the release commit, but `docs/public/schemas/` would retain the pre-release version until someone manually runs `generate-schemas` and commits.\n   443→\n   444→**Step 3.5: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` rsync exclude for `--use-head` mode**\n   445→\n   446→At line 80, the `--use-head` rsync copies the entire repo working directory into a test snapshot. The actual code is a TypeScript template literal inside an `exec()` call:\n   447→\n   448→```typescript\n   449→await exec(`rsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/`, { cwd: TEST_DIR });\n   450→```\n   451→\n   452→After migration, this would copy the entire `docs/` workspace (including `docs/node_modules/`, `docs/.next/`, etc.) into the test snapshot. While the sync script's `prepare-snapshot` step will strip `docs/` later, copying it first is wasteful and makes the test significantly slower. Add `--exclude='docs'` to the rsync. The resulting line:\n   453→\n   454→```typescript\n   455→await exec(`rsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' --exclude='docs' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/`, { cwd: TEST_DIR });\n   456→```\n   457→\n   458→The implementing agent should edit this as a TypeScript `.ts` file (it's a template literal with `${REPO_ROOT}`), not as a shell script. This is consistent with the existing pattern: `intermediates/` is also excluded from the rsync even though it would be stripped by the sync script too.\n   459→\n   460→**Step 3.6: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` `shouldNotExist`**\n   461→\n   462→At line 160, add `\"docs\"` to the verification list:\n   463→```typescript\n   464→const shouldNotExist = [\n   465→  \"intermediates\",\n   466→  \"external-docs\",\n   467→  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n   468→  \"Claude.md\",\n   469→  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n   470→  \"PUBLISHING-PLAN.md\",\n   471→  \"public-release-files\",\n   472→  \".github/actions\",\n   473→  \"scripts/sync-public.ts\",\n   474→  \"scripts/test-sync-local.ts\",\n   475→  \"scripts/release.ts\",\n   476→  \"telemetry.md\",\n   477→  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n   478→  \".github/workflows/ci.yml\",\n   479→  \"docs\",\n   480→];\n   481→```\n   482→\n   483→### Phase 4: Update `.gitignore`\n   484→\n   485→**Step 4.1: Merge docs-specific ignores into root `.gitignore`**\n   486→\n   487→Add the following to the root `.gitignore`:\n   488→\n   489→```gitignore\n   490→# Docs build output\n   491→docs/.next/\n   492→docs/out/\n   493→\n   494→# Pagefind search index (generated at build time)\n   495→docs/public/_pagefind/\n   496→```\n   497→\n   498→Most entries from the `hankweave-docs/.gitignore` (like `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`) are already covered by the root `.gitignore`. The root `.gitignore` already has generic `.next` and `out` entries (lines 141–142). In gitignore syntax, a pattern without a leading `/` or `/` in the middle matches anywhere in the directory tree. So `.next` matches both a root-level `.next/` and `docs/.next/` — this is tree-wide matching, not a coincidence. The `docs/`-prefixed additions proposed above (`docs/.next/`, `docs/out/`) are technically redundant with the existing unscoped patterns, but they make the intent explicit and are harmless.\n   499→\n   500→The Pagefind `_pagefind/` pattern is docs-specific and is not currently in the root `.gitignore`. It should be added. The root `.gitignore` already has `dist` at line 147, which would match `docs/dist/` too via tree-wide matching. No additional entry needed for that.\n   501→\n   502→Note: the `hankweave-docs/.gitignore` lives at the outer wrapper level, not inside `nextra-site/`. It is NOT copied into `docs/` during the migration (see Step 1.1). The root `.gitignore` additions above are the only gitignore changes needed.\n   503→\n   504→### Phase 5: Update documentation\n   505→\n   506→**Step 5.1: Update `standard-operating-procedures.md` -- Repository Structure (Section 1)**\n   507→\n   508→Update the directory tree at lines 10-27 to include `docs/`:\n   509→\n   510→```\n   511→hankweave-dev/              <- private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n   512→├── server/                 <- runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n   513→├── scripts/                <- build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n   514→├── tests/                  <- unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n   515→├── schemas/                <- generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n   516→├── shims/                  <- agent harness adapters\n   517→├── docs/                   <- documentation site workspace (Next.js + Nextra)\n   518→│   ├── app/                <- Next.js app directory\n   519→│   ├── content/            <- MDX documentation pages\n   520→│   ├── components/         <- custom React components\n   521→│   ├── public/             <- static assets + generated schemas\n   522→│   └── package.json        <- hankweave-docs workspace\n   523→├── learning/               <- example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n   524→├── intermediates/          <- planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n   525→├── external-docs/          <- SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n   526→├── public-release-files/   <- files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n   527→├── assets/                 <- images, demo GIFs\n   528→├── .github/                <- CI workflows and composite actions\n   529→├── CHANGELOG.md            <- Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n   530→├── CONTRIBUTING.md         <- branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n   531→└── package.json            <- @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm) + workspace root\n   532→```\n   533→\n   534→Add a note below the tree:\n   535→\n   536→```\n   537→The documentation site (`docs/`) deploys to Vercel from this repo. It is a Bun workspace\n   538→but does not have a workspace dependency on the runtime package.\n   539→```\n   540→\n   541→**Step 5.2: Update \"What gets stripped\" section (lines 31-40)**\n   542→\n   543→Add `docs/` to the directories list:\n   544→\n   545→```\n   546→- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n   547→```\n   548→\n   549→Important: the SOP's \"Directories\" list corresponds to `STRIP_DIRS` in `sync-public.ts`, while the \"Files\" list corresponds to `STRIP_FILES`. `docs` belongs in the directories list because it is added to `STRIP_DIRS`. The implementing agent should not accidentally remove or reorder existing entries (especially `CLAUDE.md`, `standard-operating-procedures.md`, etc., which are in the Files list and must remain).\n   550→\n   551→Also add a note that `\"workspaces\"` is stripped from `package.json` during public sync transformation.\n   552→\n   553→Additionally, update the prose sentence at SOP line 40 which describes the `package.json` transformation. It currently reads: \"The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\" This sentence should be updated to include the `workspaces` removal:\n   554→\n   555→```\n   556→The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`,\n   557→`homepage` points to the public repo, `release:*` scripts are removed, and the `workspaces` field is\n   558→deleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\n   559→```\n   560→\n   561→**Step 5.3: Update Key Files Reference (Section 9, lines 337-354)**\n   562→\n   563→Add docs workspace entries:\n   564→\n   565→```\n   566→| `docs/package.json`         | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)              |\n   567→| `docs/content/`             | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)      |\n   568→```\n   569→\n   570→**Step 5.4: Add Documentation site note to Development Workflow (Section 3)**\n   571→\n   572→Add a subsection:\n   573→\n   574→```markdown\n   575→### Documentation site\n   576→\n   577→The documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To work on it locally,\n   578→first ensure dependencies are installed from the repo root (`bun install`), then:\n   579→\n   580→    cd docs && bun run dev    # Development server with hot reload (uses --turbopack)\n   581→    cd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n   582→\n   583→Changes to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) deploy automatically via Vercel when\n   584→pushed to the configured branch.\n   585→\n   586→Schema generation writes to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and\n   587→`docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n   588→\n   589→    bun run generate-schemas\n   590→```\n   591→\n   592→### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n   593→\n   594→**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n   595→```bash\n   596→bun run lint\n   597→bun run tc\n   598→bun run test:unit\n   599→bun run test:integration\n   600→```\n   601→\n   602→None of these should be affected by the migration, provided the `tsconfig.json` exclude for `docs/**/*` was added in Step 2.1. Without that exclude, `bun run tc` will fail here. The runtime code is unchanged, test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/`, and Biome's `includes` whitelist only covers runtime files (not `docs/` or `scripts/`).\n   603→\n   604→**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n   605→```bash\n   606→bun run generate-schemas\n   607→```\n   608→Verify output exists in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n   609→\n   610→**Step 6.3: Run the runtime build**\n   611→```bash\n   612→bun run build\n   613→```\n   614→Verify `dist/index.js` is created. The build script uses `import.meta.dir` relative paths and `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point -- these are all unaffected by the workspace addition.\n   615→\n   616→**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n   617→```bash\n   618→cd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n   619→```\n   620→Verify Next.js build succeeds and the `postbuild` Pagefind step runs.\n   621→\n   622→**Step 6.5: Verify npm pack excludes docs**\n   623→```bash\n   624→npm pack --dry-run\n   625→```\n   626→Verify the tarball file list contains only `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`, and metadata files. The `docs/` directory should NOT appear because it's not in the `\"files\"` allowlist.\n   627→\n   628→**Step 6.6: Test the sync dry-run**\n   629→```bash\n   630→bun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <current-version> --use-head\n   631→```\n   632→Verify:\n   633→- `docs/` is correctly stripped from the snapshot\n   634→- `\"workspaces\"` is removed from the public `package.json`\n   635→- All existing verification checks pass (the `shouldNotExist` and `shouldExist` lists)\n   636→\n   637→**Step 6.7: Test release dry-run (if on develop with clean tree)**\n   638→```bash\n   639→bun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n   640→```\n   641→Verify validation passes. This exercises the merge feasibility check which now includes `\"docs/\"` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`.\n   642→\n   643→### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n   644→\n   645→This is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change.\n   646→\n   647→**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n   648→\n   649→1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`.\n   650→2. Under \"Git\" -> \"Connected Git Repository\", change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n   651→3. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`.\n   652→4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\".\n   653→5. The `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json` specifies `\"framework\": \"nextjs\"`, `\"installCommand\": \"bun install\"`, `\"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"`, and contains a `\"build\": { \"env\": { \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\" } }` flag. This is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. It is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo, where it was a deliberate team decision. **This is a human-approval gate: the implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who owns the docs Vercel deployment and knows why this flag exists.** The agent cannot make this determination on their own.\n   654→\n   655→**Step 7.2: Install behavior with `docs/` as Root Directory**\n   656→\n   657→Setting Root Directory to `docs/` means Vercel picks up `docs/vercel.json` for its configuration (installCommand, buildCommand, CVE env flag). This is important: Vercel looks for `vercel.json` at the configured Root Directory. If Root Directory were set to `/` (repo root) instead, the `docs/vercel.json` would be ignored entirely — the installCommand, buildCommand, and the CVE override flag would not apply. That is why `docs/` must be the Root Directory.\n   658→\n   659→With Root Directory set to `docs/`, Vercel checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `installCommand` (`\"bun install\"`) runs from `docs/`. Bun traverses upward from `docs/` to find the workspace root's `package.json` (which contains the `\"workspaces\"` field), then resolves and installs the full dependency tree from there. This is standard Bun workspace behavior — Bun always looks for the nearest ancestor `package.json` with a `\"workspaces\"` field.\n   660→\n   661→**If workspace resolution fails**: If `bun install` from `docs/` does not correctly find the workspace root (unlikely but not well-documented for Vercel's checkout structure), the fallback is to override `installCommand` in the Vercel dashboard to `\"cd .. && bun install\"`, which explicitly runs from the repo root. This is a dashboard-only change and does not require code modifications.\n   662→\n   663→**Alternative (repo-root approach)**: Set Root Directory to `/` and configure all commands via the Vercel dashboard (not `vercel.json`): `installCommand: \"bun install\"`, `buildCommand: \"cd docs && bun run build\"`. This guarantees workspace resolution but means `docs/vercel.json` is not used — all settings including the CVE env flag must be replicated in the Vercel dashboard. This approach is more fragile because dashboard settings are not version-controlled and the CVE flag could be silently lost.\n   664→\n   665→**Step 7.3: Configure \"Ignored Build Step\" (recommended)**\n   666→\n   667→To prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n   668→1. Go to Settings -> General -> \"Ignored Build Step\"\n   669→2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n   670→\n   671→This tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n   672→\n   673→**Caveat**: Vercel may perform shallow clones for deployments. If `HEAD^` is not available in a shallow clone, this command will fail. In that case, Vercel would proceed with the build (the command exits non-zero, which Vercel treats as \"do not skip\"). This is a safe failure mode — Vercel builds unnecessarily rather than skipping a needed build. If this becomes a problem (frequent unnecessary builds), the more robust alternative is to use Vercel's environment variable approach: a custom script that checks `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` against the previous deployment.\n   674→\n   675→**Step 7.4: Configure branch deployment**\n   676→\n   677→The monorepo uses `develop` and `release/alpha` branches. Configure Vercel:\n   678→- Production deployment: whichever branch the docs should deploy from (likely `develop` or `release/alpha`, depending on team preference)\n   679→- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`\n   680→\n   681→### Phase 8: Commit, PR, and cleanup\n   682→\n   683→**Step 8.1: Commit the migration**\n   684→\n   685→Commit all changes on the feature branch. Ensure the regenerated `bun.lock` is staged — without it, CI's `bun install --frozen-lockfile` will fail on the first run after the PR is opened.\n   686→\n   687→```bash\n   688→git add bun.lock\n   689→```\n   690→\n   691→Commit message:\n   692→```\n   693→Migrate to monorepo: bring docs into workspace\n   694→\n   695→- Add docs/ workspace (formerly SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs)\n   696→- Add \"workspaces\" field to root package.json\n   697→- Regenerate bun.lock as unified workspace lockfile\n   698→- Exclude docs/**/* from root tsconfig.json\n   699→- Update generate-schemas.ts to use monorepo-local docs path\n   700→- Add docs/ to strip lists (sync-public.ts, release.ts, test-sync-local.ts)\n   701→- Strip \"workspaces\" from public package.json in transformPackageJson()\n   702→- Update release.ts schema handling for docs/public/schemas/\n   703→- Add --exclude='docs' to test-sync-local.ts rsync\n   704→- Update .gitignore for docs build artifacts\n   705→- Update standard-operating-procedures.md\n   706→```\n   707→\n   708→Note on merge convention: per SOP Section 4, the repo uses squash merges. When the PR is squash-merged into `develop`, only the PR title becomes the commit message on `develop`. The bullet-point list above is for the feature branch record and the PR body — it will not appear verbatim in `develop`'s git log.\n   709→\n   710→**Step 8.2: Open PR targeting develop**\n   711→\n   712→Follow the existing PR conventions per SOP Section 4:\n   713→- Title: `Monorepo migration: consolidate hankweave-docs into workspace`\n   714→- Body: Problem/approach/files-changed format\n   715→\n   716→**Step 8.3: Optional CHANGELOG entry**\n   717→\n   718→Under `[Unreleased] > Changed`:\n   719→```markdown\n   720→- **Monorepo migration** -- Consolidated documentation site into the development repository as a Bun workspace. No changes to runtime behavior or public-facing interfaces.\n   721→```\n   722→\n   723→This is optional since it's an internal infrastructure change, not user-facing.\n   724→\n   725→**Step 8.4: Archive the old `hankweave-docs` repo (after migration is fully verified)**\n   726→\n   727→This step happens last — after the PR is merged, CI passes on the merged branch, and the Vercel deployment from the monorepo is confirmed working. Do not archive the old repo before these are all green.\n   728→\n   729→1. Archive `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` on GitHub (set to read-only).\n   730→2. Update the README to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave.\"\n   731→3. Disconnect Vercel from the old repo (after the new connection is verified).\n   732→\n   733→---\n   734→\n   735→## g. Testing Considerations\n   736→\n   737→### Existing tests affected\n   738→\n   739→**None.** No existing test file needs modification. The migration changes the root `package.json` (adds `\"workspaces\"`), `tsconfig.json` (adds `\"docs/**/*\"` to exclude), scripts in `scripts/` (path adjustments, strip list updates), `.gitignore`, and the SOP document. None of these are read by tests.\n   740→\n   741→Specifically checked:\n   742→- `tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts` reads from `schemas/` which is unchanged.\n   743→- `tests/unit/config.test.ts` reads from `server/config.ts` which is unchanged.\n   744→- `tests/unit/utils.test.ts` uses `getMetadata()` which reads version from `package.json` -- the `\"workspaces\"` field won't affect this.\n   745→- All test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/` which are unaffected.\n   746→\n   747→### New tests needed\n   748→\n   749→No new automated tests are strictly required. The migration is a structural change, not a code change. Verification is manual (Phase 6 above).\n   750→\n   751→Optional: A lightweight monorepo smoke test could be added:\n   752→```typescript\n   753→// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\n   754→import { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\n   755→import * as fs from \"node:fs\";\n   756→import * as path from \"node:path\";\n   757→\n   758→describe(\"monorepo structure\", () => {\n   759→  const ROOT = path.resolve(import.meta.dir, \"../..\");\n   760→\n   761→  test(\"root package.json declares docs workspace\", () => {\n   762→    const pkg = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, \"package.json\"), \"utf8\"));\n   763→    expect(pkg.workspaces).toContain(\"docs\");\n   764→  });\n   765→\n   766→  test(\"docs workspace has package.json\", () => {\n   767→    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"package.json\"))).toBe(true);\n   768→  });\n   769→\n   770→  test(\"docs public directory exists\", () => {\n   771→    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"public\"))).toBe(true);\n   772→  });\n   773→});\n   774→```\n   775→\n   776→Note: An earlier version of this test checked `docs/public/schemas/` existence, but that directory is only created by `generate-schemas` and may not exist on a fresh checkout. The test now checks `docs/public/` instead, which is always present in the repo. If the team wants a schema-existence test, it should be in the schema generation test suite, not a structural smoke test.\n   777→\n   778→This is a small structural invariant check. Only add if the team wants structural tests.\n   779→\n   780→### Edge cases to cover\n   781→\n   782→1. **`bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI.** The new unified `bun.lock` must be committed. If it's missing, CI's `--frozen-lockfile` flag will fail. This is the composite action at `.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n   783→\n   784→2. **npm publish from root.** The `\"files\"` field (`[\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]`) is an allowlist. `docs/` is not listed, so it won't be in the npm tarball. Verify with `npm pack --dry-run`.\n   785→\n   786→3. **Dependency hoisting conflicts.** Bun workspaces hoists dependencies. The runtime and docs have almost entirely different dependency trees (server-side Bun tools vs. React/Next.js), so conflicts are unlikely. The only shared dev dependency is `typescript`, which can safely have independent versions per workspace.\n   787→\n   788→4. **Build executable.** `build-executable.ts` uses `ENTRY_POINT = \"server/index.ts\"` and builds from root. This path doesn't change with workspace addition.\n   789→\n   790→5. **Cross-runtime tests.** `run-cross-runtime-tests.ts` resolves `testsDir` from `import.meta.dirname`. This path doesn't change.\n   791→\n   792→6. **Cross-platform path separators.** The CI matrix includes Windows, macOS, and Linux. Workspace paths are resolved by Bun which handles path separators. No issues expected, but worth monitoring the first CI run after migration.\n   793→\n   794→7. **Root `tsconfig.json` include scope.** The `include: [\"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\"]` glob picks up everything in the repo tree. Without `\"docs/**/*\"` in `exclude`, `bun run tc` will fail on docs files (incompatible compiler options). This is addressed in Phase 2, Step 2.1, but listed here as an edge case because it is easy to miss in future workspace additions — any new workspace with TypeScript files will need the same exclude entry.\n   795→\n   796→8. **Biome scope does not cover scripts.** The `biome.json` `includes` list covers `server/**/*`, `tests/e2e/**/*`, `tests/utils/**/*`, `tests/unit/**/*`, and `types/**/*`. The `scripts/` directory is not included. This means the changes to `sync-public.ts`, `release.ts`, `test-sync-local.ts`, and `generate-schemas.ts` will not be linted by Biome. This is existing behavior, not a regression — just noting it so the implementing agent does not expect Biome to catch formatting issues in modified scripts.\n   797→\n   798→9. **`bunfig.toml` workspace scope.** The root `bunfig.toml` has only a `[test.env]` section setting `BUN_TEST = \"true\"`. This env var is consumed by `isNonInteractive()` in the runtime's `execution-setup.ts` to skip confirmation prompts during tests. It applies to `bun test` runs from the root, but all test scripts (`test:unit`, `test:integration`, etc.) are path-scoped to specific directories (`tests/unit`, `tests/integration`, etc.) and will not pick up docs files. No `bunfig.toml` changes are needed. The implementing agent should verify this remains true if the docs workspace ever adds its own test scripts.\n   799→\n   800→10. **`generate-schemas` becomes a hard failure if `docs/public/` is absent.** Before migration, if the sibling docs repo wasn't cloned, `docsAvailable` was `false` and the script silently skipped writing to docs. After migration, `docs/public/` is always present in a normal checkout, so `docsAvailable` is always `true`. If someone runs the script in an unusual environment where `docs/` is absent (sparse checkout, Docker build that mounts only `server/` and `schemas/`), the script will attempt to write to `docs/public/schemas/` and the filesystem write will fail — a hard error where it was previously a silent skip. This is acceptable for normal development but worth documenting. If the team runs `generate-schemas` in CI or Docker builds that don't check out the full repo, they should either ensure `docs/` is present or add explicit error handling.\n   801→\n   802→### CI enhancement (optional, recommended)\n   803→\n   804→Consider adding a docs build step to CI to prevent monorepo PRs from silently breaking docs:\n   805→\n   806→```yaml\n   807→# In ci.yml, add a new job:\n   808→docs-build:\n   809→  name: Docs Build Check\n   810→  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n   811→  timeout-minutes: 10\n   812→  steps:\n   813→    - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n   814→    - uses: ./.github/actions/setup-environment\n   815→      with:\n   816→        install-gemini: \"false\"\n   817→    - name: Build docs\n   818→      run: cd docs && bun run build\n   819→```\n   820→\n   821→This is a nice-to-have. Without it, a broken docs build would only be caught by Vercel deployment failures, not by the PR CI checks. Note that the docs build will run the `postbuild` pagefind step (`pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind`), which scans the full Next.js server output. The CI job may take longer than a typical build check because of this. The output goes to `docs/public/_pagefind/`, which is gitignored — it does not need to be persisted.\n   822→\n   823→---\n   824→\n   825→## h. Documentation Considerations\n   826→\n   827→### Docs to update\n   828→\n   829→1. **`standard-operating-procedures.md`** -- Repository structure diagram, strip list documentation, key files reference, development workflow section. Detailed in Phase 5 above.\n   830→2. **This plan itself** -- Should be filed as an intermediate (e.g., `intermediates/45-monorepo-migration/`).\n   831→\n   832→### New docs needed\n   833→\n   834→None required. The monorepo migration is an internal infrastructure change. The user-facing docs (`docs/content/`) document Hankweave as a CLI tool, not the development repo structure.\n   835→\n   836→### Docs that should NOT be updated\n   837→\n   838→- User-facing docs (`docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`, etc.) -- these reference `hankweave` as a CLI tool, not a repo layout.\n   839→- The public `README.md` (in `public-release-files/`) -- describes the public `hankweave-runtime` repo.\n   840→- The `CHANGELOG.md` -- unless the team wants the optional internal note described in Phase 8.\n   841→\n   842→---\n   843→\n   844→## i. Additional Resources\n   845→\n   846→### Bun workspaces\n   847→\n   848→- [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) -- primary reference\n   849→- Root package can be a workspace member itself (important for the \"runtime at root\" approach)\n   850→- `bun install` at root installs all workspace dependencies\n   851→- `bun --filter <name> <script>` runs scripts in specific workspaces (useful for `bun --filter hankweave-docs build`)\n   852→\n   853→### Vercel monorepo support\n   854→\n   855→- Vercel's \"Root Directory\" setting specifies which subdirectory contains the deployable project\n   856→- \"Ignored Build Step\" filters deploys to only relevant changes\n   857→- Vercel handles Bun workspaces when it can see the full repo tree\n   858→\n   859→### Git subtree (optional history preservation)\n   860→\n   861→```bash\n   862→git remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\n   863→git merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\n   864→git read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\n   865→git commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n   866→```\n   867→\n   868→This preserves the full commit history of `hankweave-docs` at the `docs/` prefix. After this, restructure so the `nextra-site/` contents are at `docs/` root and remove unwanted wrapper files.\n   869→\n   870→---\n   871→\n   872→## Summary of Changes\n   873→\n   874→| File | Change Type | Description |\n   875→|------|-------------|-------------|\n   876→| `package.json` | Modify | Add `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` (after `\"engines\"`) |\n   877→| `tsconfig.json` | Modify | Add `\"docs/**/*\"` to `exclude` array |\n   878→| `docs/` | Create (directory) | Contains flattened contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` |\n   879→| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Modify | Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (lines 24-32); update comment on `docsAvailable` (line 75) |\n   880→| `scripts/sync-public.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` (line 28); add `delete pkg.workspaces` to `transformPackageJson()` |\n   881→| `scripts/release.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs/\"` to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` (line 54); extend schema diff check to cover `docs/public/schemas/` (line 427); extend schema revert to cover `docs/public/schemas/` (line 436); extend `git add` to include `docs/public/schemas/` (line 510) |\n   882→| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | Modify | Add `--exclude='docs'` to `--use-head` rsync (line 80); add `\"docs\"` to `shouldNotExist` (line 160) |\n   883→| `.gitignore` | Modify | Add `docs/public/_pagefind/` and scoped `docs/.next/`, `docs/out/` patterns (technically redundant with existing unscoped patterns but explicit) |\n   884→| `standard-operating-procedures.md` | Modify | Update repo structure, strip list, key files, package.json transform description (line 40), add docs section |\n   885→| `bun.lock` | Regenerate | New unified lockfile covering both workspaces — must be staged and committed (Step 8.1) |\n   886→\n   887→**Total files modified**: 8 existing files + 1 new directory (~30 files from docs)\n   888→**Total lines of code changed**: ~35 lines across scripts and configs + SOP update\n   889→\n   890→The migration's success is measured by how little changes while still achieving the goal: a reliable, explicit relationship between runtime and docs in a single repo, with the public sync pipeline continuing to produce correct output.\n   891→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"774524ca-ef80-4770-b634-d06c9d7eeed4","uuid":"72a31f3d-3a54-4391-bd52-67b3ea24b9ae","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/merged/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Migrate Hankweave to Monorepo\n\n## a. The Main Task\n\nConsolidate the `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` (runtime) and `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` (documentation site) repositories into a single monorepo using Bun workspaces. The runtime package stays at the repository root. The docs site moves in as a workspace at `docs/`, flattened from its current `nextra-site/` subdirectory. The existing CI/CD pipelines, release process, public sync workflow, and Vercel docs deployment continue to function with targeted, minimal changes.\n\n---\n\n## b. Information Available\n\n### Sources that informed this plan\n\n1. **Task specification** -- `plan/task.md` (task description, open questions, research on Bun workspaces, Turborepo, and git subtree merges).\n2. **Opus plan and addendums** -- `plan/rough-workspace/opus/plan.md`, `addendum-docs.md`, `addendum-tests.md`, `log.md`. Detailed, phase-by-phase plan with exact file diffs, SOP drafts, and edge case analysis.\n3. **Codex plan and addendums** -- `plan/rough-workspace/codex/plan.md`, `addendum-1.md` (tests/CI), `addendum-2.md` (docs/deployment). Shorter plan with a notable catch on the `transformPackageJson()` gap for stripping `\"workspaces\"` from the public `package.json`.\n4. **Full source code review** -- Every config, script, workflow, and representative server/test file from both `source/hankweave/` and `source/hankweave-docs/`.\n\n### What I could see\n\n- Full directory structure and file contents of both repos.\n- All CI workflows (ci.yml, release.yml, sync-public.yml), composite actions (setup-environment, run-tests, dump-test-logs), and scripts (build.ts, build-executable.ts, release.ts, sync-public.ts, generate-schemas.ts, test-sync-local.ts, e2e/init.ts, run-cross-runtime-tests.ts).\n- Both `package.json` files, both `tsconfig.json` files, `biome.json`, `bunfig.toml`.\n- The full docs Nextra/Next.js setup including `vercel.json`, `next.config.mjs`, `app/`, `content/`, `components/`, `public/`, `styles/`.\n- The SOP document (`standard-operating-procedures.md`).\n- The `.gitignore` files from both repos.\n\n### What I could NOT see\n\n- The actual GitHub repos (branch protection rules, secrets, default branches, collaborator list).\n- The Vercel project dashboard settings (current root directory config, branch deployment rules).\n- The `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime` (public mirror) repo contents -- only references to it in scripts and workflows.\n- Linear project / ticket context (no ticket ID provided).\n- Whether there are other repos or tools beyond what's in the source snapshot that the task spec's \"related packages/tools\" refers to.\n\n### Assumptions\n\n1. The `hankweave-docs` repo does not have significant git history worth preserving. The task spec describes history preservation as \"nice to have.\" The default approach is a simple file copy with a descriptive commit. The git subtree method is documented in Section i as an optional escape hatch only — not the recommended path.\n2. The public mirror (`hankweave-runtime`) continues to exist as a separate repo. This migration consolidates the two private development repos only.\n3. The docs site continues to deploy to Vercel, now from the monorepo.\n4. No Turborepo or other task orchestration is needed for two packages.\n5. The `hankweave-docs/` outer wrapper (README, `notes/`, `plan/`) is development scaffolding that does not need to be carried into the monorepo.\n\n---\n\n## c. Sources and Related Things\n\n### Original task\n\n- Task description: `plan/task.md`\n- No Linear ticket ID provided.\n\n### Code references\n\n| File | Line(s) | Relevance |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `source/hankweave/package.json` | Full | Root package.json -- needs `workspaces` field added |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/package.json` | Full | Docs package.json -- moves to `docs/package.json` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 24-32 | The only cross-repo code dependency; `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | 76 | `docsAvailable` check -- will become permanently true |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 28 | `STRIP_DIRS` -- needs `\"docs\"` added |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/sync-public.ts` | 173-196 | `transformPackageJson()` -- needs to strip `\"workspaces\"` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 54-60 | `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` -- needs `\"docs/\"` added |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 436 | Schema revert in validation -- needs to also revert `docs/public/schemas/` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/release.ts` | 510 | Release commit `git add` -- needs to also add `docs/public/schemas/` |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 80 | `--use-head` rsync -- needs `--exclude='docs'` added |\n| `source/hankweave/scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | 160-175 | `shouldNotExist` verification list -- needs `\"docs\"` added |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/ci.yml` | Full | CI workflow -- minimal changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/release.yml` | Full | Release workflow -- minimal changes |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/workflows/sync-public.yml` | Full | Sync workflow -- no code changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml` | 34 | `bun install --frozen-lockfile` -- works with workspaces |\n| `source/hankweave/biome.json` | 4-11 | Linting scope whitelist -- only covers runtime files |\n| `source/hankweave/bunfig.toml` | Full | Bun config -- no changes needed |\n| `source/hankweave/tsconfig.json` | Full | Runtime TS config -- needs `\"docs/**/*\"` added to `exclude` |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/tsconfig.json` | Full | Docs TS config -- stays as-is at new location |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/vercel.json` | Full | Vercel config -- stays in docs workspace |\n| `source/hankweave-docs/nextra-site/next.config.mjs` | Full | Next.js config -- no changes |\n| `source/hankweave/standard-operating-procedures.md` | 10-30, 31-40, 337-354 | Repo structure, strip list docs, key files |\n| `source/hankweave/.gitignore` | Full | May need additions for docs build artifacts |\n| `source/hankweave/server/utils.ts` | 402-410 | `getMetadata()` reads `../package.json` -- structural assumption |\n\n### Research sources\n\n- [Bun Workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) -- native workspace support via `\"workspaces\"` field in `package.json`\n- [Bun Catalogs](https://bun.sh/docs/install/catalogs) -- optional shared dependency versioning (not needed initially)\n- [Git subtree merges](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/using-git/about-git-subtree-merges) -- for preserving docs repo history\n\n---\n\n## d. Conventions and Patterns\n\n### Observed code conventions\n\n1. **Scripts are TypeScript.** All build/release/sync scripts are `.ts` files run with `bun`.\n2. **Relative paths from `import.meta.dir`.** Scripts use `path.join(import.meta.dir, \"..\")` to resolve project root.\n3. **`Bun.spawn` for shell commands.** Scripts use `Bun.spawn([\"sh\", \"-c\", cmd])`. Note: each script defines its own `exec()` helper with different signatures. `sync-public.ts` has `exec(cmd, { cwd?, silent? })`, while `release.ts` has `exec(command, { silent?, allowFailure? })`. These are not shared. The implementing agent should not assume one script's `exec()` options exist in the other.\n4. **Two-space indent, 100-char line width.** Enforced by Biome.\n5. **LF line endings.** Enforced by `.gitattributes`.\n6. **No shared state between packages.** Docs don't import from runtime; only schema JSON files are copied by a script.\n7. **Strip lists are explicit.** Both `sync-public.ts` and `release.ts` maintain explicit lists of files/dirs to remove. Adding a new internal directory means adding it to both.\n8. **Runtime is strict ESM TypeScript** with explicit `.js` import specifiers (e.g., `import \"./utils.js\"`).\n9. **`server/utils.ts` reads `../package.json`** for version metadata. This assumes `server/` is one level below the package root.\n\n### Patterns to follow\n\n- Add `\"docs\"` to existing strip lists rather than creating new mechanisms.\n- Keep the root `package.json` as the runtime's `package.json` (add only the `\"workspaces\"` field).\n- Don't introduce Turborepo or other tooling -- Bun workspaces is sufficient for two packages.\n- Keep separate `tsconfig.json` per workspace (they have incompatible settings: runtime targets ES2022 with strict mode and bun-types; docs targets ES2020 with strict disabled and Next.js plugin).\n- Don't introduce `workspace:*` dependencies between docs and runtime. The schema copy is a script-level file operation, not a package dependency.\n\n### Patterns to avoid\n\n- Don't move the runtime into a subdirectory. This would break dozens of paths across scripts, CI workflows, and `server/utils.ts` metadata resolution.\n- Don't create a shared `tsconfig.base.json`. The two configs are too different.\n- Don't add workspace filter flags to CI scripts. For two packages, let everything install and run from root.\n- Don't extend `biome.json` to cover `docs/`. The docs site uses React/JSX conventions and Next.js tooling that are not part of the runtime's Biome setup.\n\n---\n\n## e. Decision Points and Judgement Calls\n\n### Decision 1: Keep the runtime at the repo root\n\n**Chosen**: The runtime stays at root. The docs site moves into `docs/`.\n\n**Reasoning**: The runtime has 8 scripts, 3 CI workflows, 3 composite actions, build/release/sync pipelines, and extensive relative path usage (scripts use `import.meta.dir` + `\"..\"`, `server/utils.ts` reads `../package.json`, build scripts use `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point). Moving it into a subdirectory would require changing every one of these. The docs site has no CI of its own, no complex scripts, and a single `package.json`.\n\n**Trade-off**: The root `package.json` serves dual purpose as both workspace root and runtime package. This is a recognized Bun workspace pattern -- the root package is an \"implicit workspace member\" by default.\n\n**Alternative considered**: Both packages in `packages/` subdirectories. This is the conventional monorepo layout but would be a massive path-rewrite refactor (build scripts, CI workflows, composite actions, `import.meta.dir` references, `server/utils.ts` metadata resolution). Not justified for two packages.\n\n### Decision 2: Flatten the docs into `docs/`\n\n**Chosen**: Move `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/*` contents directly into `docs/` (not `docs/nextra-site/` or `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/`).\n\n**Reasoning**: The `nextra-site/` wrapper exists because the docs repo has a root with README, `notes/`, and `plan/` directories. In the monorepo, these don't need a separate layer. Flattening gives a clean workspace path (`\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`), shorter references everywhere, and follows common conventions where `docs/` is a well-understood directory name.\n\n**Alternative considered**: Keeping `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` intact (the Codex recommendation). This preserves the original structure verbatim but results in the awkward path `\"workspaces\": [\"hankweave-docs/nextra-site\"]` and an unnecessary depth of nesting. Every reference to the docs workspace becomes longer.\n\n**What happens to `hankweave-docs/` non-nextra files**: The `README.md` at docs repo root is optional and can be included as `docs/README.md` or discarded. The `notes/` and `plan/` directories are historical development artifacts -- discard them. If the team wants them preserved, archive in `intermediates/` (e.g., `intermediates/docs-migration-legacy/`).\n\n### Decision 3: No `workspace:*` dependencies\n\n**Chosen**: No workspace dependency between docs and runtime.\n\n**Reasoning**: The docs site doesn't `import` anything from `@southbridgeai/hankweave`. The only data flow is schema JSON files copied by `generate-schemas.ts`. This is a script-level file copy, not a package dependency. Adding `workspace:*` would incorrectly suggest the docs depend on the runtime as a library.\n\n**When to revisit**: If the docs ever need to import TypeScript types or utilities from the runtime (e.g., for auto-generated API reference), add `workspace:*` at that point.\n\n### Decision 4: Convert the existing hankweave repo, don't create new\n\n**Chosen**: Modify `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave` in-place to become the monorepo.\n\n**Reasoning**: All CI workflows, GitHub secrets, branch protection rules, Linear integration, and team permissions are already configured on this repo. Creating a new repo would require reconfiguring everything. The existing git history stays intact. The docs files are brought in via a merge commit (or `git subtree add` if history preservation is desired).\n\n### Decision 5: No Turborepo\n\n**Chosen**: Use Bun workspaces alone.\n\n**Reasoning**: Two packages don't need task orchestration or caching. The runtime builds and tests independently. The docs site builds independently on Vercel. There's no dependency chain requiring `^build` semantics. Adding Turborepo would increase complexity with no clear benefit.\n\n**When to revisit**: If more packages are added (e.g., a shared types package, a CLI package), or if CI times become problematic and caching would help.\n\n### Decision 6: Single lockfile at root\n\n**Chosen**: One `bun.lock` at the repo root, replacing both existing lockfiles.\n\n**Reasoning**: Bun workspaces uses a single root lockfile. Both workspace packages' dependencies are resolved and locked together. The first `bun install` after migration regenerates this. The docs-side `bun.lock` (from `nextra-site/`) is deleted.\n\n### Decision 7: Strip `\"workspaces\"` from public `package.json`\n\n**Chosen**: Modify `transformPackageJson()` in `sync-public.ts` to delete `pkg.workspaces` before writing the public `package.json`.\n\n**Reasoning**: The public mirror will not contain the `docs/` directory (it's stripped by `STRIP_DIRS`). If the public `package.json` ships with `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]`, it references a directory that doesn't exist, which is confusing and could cause unexpected behavior when someone runs `bun install` or `npm install` on the public repo. This was identified by the Codex plan and confirmed by code review -- the current `transformPackageJson()` function (at `sync-public.ts:173-196`) changes `name`, `publishConfig`, `homepage`, and strips some scripts, but does not touch `workspaces`.\n\n### Over/under engineering line\n\n- **Under (acceptable)**: Not adding `paths:` filtering to CI triggers. Docs changes trigger runtime tests, but CI runs are fast enough that this isn't worth the complexity for two packages.\n- **Under (acceptable)**: Not using Bun catalogs for shared TypeScript version. The projects only share TypeScript as a dev dependency and can version independently.\n- **Over (avoid)**: Adding Turborepo, shared tsconfig base, workspace link scripts, or monorepo-specific CI orchestration tooling.\n- **Right level**: Adding `\"docs\"` to strip lists, updating one path in `generate-schemas.ts`, updating the SOP, stripping `\"workspaces\"` from public package.json. Minimal changes that make the monorepo work correctly.\n\n---\n\n## f. The Actual Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Preparation\n\n**Step 0.1: Create a feature branch**\n```bash\ngit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b hrishi/monorepo-migration\n```\n\n**Step 0.2: Verify clean state**\n```bash\nbun run test:unit\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\ngit status  # must be clean\n```\n\n### Phase 1: Bring docs into the repo\n\n**Step 1.1: Create the `docs/` directory**\n\nBefore copying, verify that no `docs/` directory already exists in the repo (e.g., from a prior attempt). If it does, remove it first or abort and investigate. The source snapshot confirms `docs/` does not currently exist in `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n\nCopy the contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` into `docs/` at the runtime repo root. The files to bring in:\n\n```\napp/\ncomponents/\ncontent/\npublic/\nstyles/\nbun.lock           (will be removed in step 1.3)\nmdx-components.tsx\nnext-env.d.ts\nnext.config.mjs\npackage.json\ntsconfig.json\nvercel.json\n```\n\nDo NOT copy the outer `hankweave-docs/` wrapper (`notes/`, `plan/`). Optionally include `hankweave-docs/README.md` as `docs/README.md` for internal reference.\n\nNote: the `hankweave-docs` repo's `.gitignore` lives at the outer wrapper level (`hankweave-docs/.gitignore`), not inside `nextra-site/`. Since we are copying `nextra-site/*` into `docs/`, no `.gitignore` is carried over. The root `.gitignore` already covers the relevant patterns (see Phase 4).\n\nIf git history preservation is desired, use `git subtree add`:\n```bash\ngit remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\ngit merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\ngit read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\ngit commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n```\nThen restructure: move `docs/nextra-site/*` up to `docs/` and remove `docs/notes/`, `docs/plan/`, `docs/README.md`, `docs/.gitignore`.\n\nAlternatively, a simple file copy with a descriptive commit is simpler and the docs repo history remains available in the original repo if ever needed.\n\n**Step 1.2: Add workspace configuration to root `package.json`**\n\nAdd the `\"workspaces\"` field to `source/hankweave/package.json`. Place it after `\"engines\"` and before `\"scripts\"`, adjacent to other structural metadata fields. The current field order is: `name`, `version`, `description`, `author`, `keywords`, `homepage`, `type`, `publishConfig`, `bin`, `main`, `files`, `engines`, `scripts`, `devDependencies`, `peerDependencies`, `dependencies`. The result:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"@southbridgeai/hankweave\",\n  \"version\": \"0.5.6\",\n  ...\n  \"engines\": { \"node\": \">=20.0.0\" },\n  \"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],\n  \"scripts\": { ... },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe actual edit is a single line addition: insert `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"],` between the closing `}` of `\"engines\"` and the opening `\"scripts\"` key.\n\nThe root package is itself an implicit workspace member in Bun workspaces, so the runtime's own dependencies, scripts, and `\"files\"` field continue to work unchanged.\n\n**Step 1.3: Delete the docs-side `bun.lock`**\n\nRemove `docs/bun.lock` with a plain filesystem `rm docs/bun.lock`. This file was just copied in Step 1.1 and has not been committed to git yet, so `git rm` is not needed. The root lockfile will manage all dependencies after `bun install` in Step 1.4.\n\n**Step 1.4: Run `bun install`**\n\nFrom the repo root:\n```bash\nbun install\n```\n\nThis resolves dependencies for both workspaces, generates a new unified `bun.lock` at root, and creates a single hoisted `node_modules/` tree. The docs dependencies (`next`, `nextra`, `react`, `posthog-js`, etc.) will be added to the root lockfile but should not conflict with the runtime's dependencies — the two packages have almost entirely different dependency trees.\n\nCheck the `bun install` output for warnings. One potential friction point: the root `package.json` has `\"peerDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.9.2\" }` while the docs `package.json` has `\"devDependencies\": { \"typescript\": \"^5.0.0\" }`. Bun may emit a peer dependency mismatch warning. This is cosmetic — it does not affect the build — but if it does, the docs `typescript` range could be narrowed to `\"^5.9.2\"` to match.\n\n**Step 1.5: Verify docs build locally**\n\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n```\n\nVerify the Next.js build succeeds. The `postbuild` step (Pagefind search indexing) should also run. If the build fails, the most likely cause is module resolution issues due to dependency hoisting. Bun workspaces hoist dependencies to the root `node_modules/`. The docs' dependencies (`next`, `nextra`, `react`, etc.) will land in the root `node_modules/`, not `docs/node_modules/`. ESM imports from `docs/next.config.mjs` (e.g., `import nextra from 'nextra'`) should resolve via Node's upward traversal, but if they don't, the fallback is to run `bun install` explicitly from `docs/` to create a local `node_modules/` with symlinks.\n\n### Phase 2: Update cross-repo references\n\n**Step 2.1: Exclude `docs/` from root `tsconfig.json`**\n\nThe root `tsconfig.json` has `\"include\": [\"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\"]` which will pick up every TypeScript and TSX file in the repository. After migration, this glob matches `docs/**/*.ts` and `docs/**/*.tsx` — files that are incompatible with the runtime's compiler options. The docs workspace uses `\"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\"`, `\"module\": \"esnext\"`, `\"jsx\": \"preserve\"`, and `\"strict\": false`, while the runtime uses `\"moduleResolution\": \"node\"`, `\"module\": \"ES2022\"`, no JSX, and `\"strict\": true`. Running `bun run tc` (which invokes `tsc --noEmit` with the root tsconfig) will fail on docs files unless they are excluded.\n\nAdd `\"docs/**/*\"` to the `exclude` array in the root `tsconfig.json`:\n\n```json\n\"exclude\": [\n  \"node_modules\",\n  \".logs/**/*\",\n  \"intermediates/**/*\",\n  \"learning/**/*\",\n  \"tests/test-area/**/*\",\n  \"docs/**/*\"\n]\n```\n\nThis is a required change. Without it, the migration will break `bun run tc`, which is run in CI and during release validation.\n\n**Step 2.2: Update `scripts/generate-schemas.ts`**\n\nChange the `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (lines 24-32) from:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"..\",\n  \"hankweave-docs\",\n  \"nextra-site\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nTo:\n```typescript\nconst DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR = path.join(\n  import.meta.dir,\n  \"..\",\n  \"docs\",\n  \"public\",\n  \"schemas\",\n);\n```\n\nThe `docsAvailable` check at line 76 (`fs.existsSync(path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR))`) will now always evaluate to true since `docs/public/` is always present in the monorepo. Note the precision here: `path.dirname(DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR)` resolves to `docs/public/` (the parent of `schemas/`), not `docs/` itself. If `docs/public/` exists but `docs/public/schemas/` does not, the `mkdirSync` at line 78 creates it — that is correct behavior.\n\nKeep the check for defensive safety — it doesn't hurt, and it means the script still works correctly if someone runs it from an unexpected working directory (e.g., sparse checkout). Update the comment at line 75 to reflect the new context. The existing comment says \"optional - only used if hosting schemas on hankweave.dev\" — it should say something like \"Always available in monorepo; guard checks docs/public/ existence and is kept for safety in edge cases (sparse checkout, unusual working directory).\" The silent-skip behavior means a misconfiguration would produce no error and simply not write schemas to docs — worth being aware of, though unlikely in normal operation.\n\n**Step 2.3: Run schema generation to verify**\n\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\nls schemas/hank.schema.json\nls docs/public/schemas/hank.schema.json\n```\n\nVerify schemas are written to both `schemas/` (runtime's local copy for npm) and `docs/public/schemas/` (docs site's static files).\n\n### Phase 3: Update strip lists for public sync\n\nThis phase has five changes across three scripts, plus an update to the `transformPackageJson()` function. All serve the same purpose: ensure the docs workspace doesn't leak into the public mirror and that schema handling covers both output directories.\n\n**Step 3.1: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` `STRIP_DIRS`**\n\nAt line 28, add `\"docs\"`:\n```typescript\nconst STRIP_DIRS = [\"intermediates\", \"external-docs\", \"public-release-files\", \".github/actions\", \"docs\"];\n```\n\nThis single constant change covers two consumers in the script: `prepareSnapshot()` (line 214, which builds rsync excludes with trailing slashes from `STRIP_DIRS`) and `initialSync()` (lines 291–302, which iterates `STRIP_DIRS` to `rm -rf` each directory from the public clone). `snapshotSync()` does not reference `STRIP_DIRS` directly — it copies the pre-built snapshot directory, which was already stripped by `prepareSnapshot()`. The implementing agent does not need to touch any of these functions — they all operate from the shared `STRIP_DIRS` constant or its output.\n\n**Step 3.2: Update `scripts/sync-public.ts` `transformPackageJson()`**\n\nAt line 173-196 in the `transformPackageJson()` function, add a line to delete the `\"workspaces\"` field from the public `package.json`:\n\n```typescript\nasync function transformPackageJson(packagePath: string): Promise<void> {\n  const content = await readFile(packagePath, \"utf-8\");\n  const pkg = JSON.parse(content);\n\n  pkg.name = \"hankweave\";\n  pkg.publishConfig = { access: \"public\" };\n  pkg.homepage = \"https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime\";\n\n  // Remove monorepo-only fields that don't apply to the public repo\n  delete pkg.workspaces;\n\n  // Remove scripts that reference stripped internal files\n  if (pkg.scripts) {\n    for (const script of STRIP_SCRIPTS) {\n      delete pkg.scripts[script];\n    }\n  }\n\n  await writeFile(packagePath, JSON.stringify(pkg, null, 2) + \"\\n\");\n  // ... logging\n}\n```\n\nThe code snippet above shows the full function for context, but the actual edit is minimal: insert `delete pkg.workspaces;` (with a comment) on a new line between line 179 (`pkg.homepage = ...`) and line 181 (`// Remove scripts...`). There is a blank line 180 between these — the insert goes there. Do not rewrite the entire function — the existing `console.log` lines at 189–195 are unchanged.\n\nThis is critical. Without it, the public `package.json` would contain `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` pointing to a directory that doesn't exist in the public mirror.\n\n**Step 3.3: Update `scripts/release.ts` `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`**\n\nAt line 54, add `\"docs/\"`:\n```typescript\nconst INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP = [\n  \"intermediates/\",\n  \"external-docs/\",\n  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n  \"docs/\",\n];\n```\n\nThis ensures the docs workspace is stripped from `release/alpha` during the release process (the `develop -> release/alpha` merge step). An important subtlety: the release script already has logic (at lines 366-385) to auto-resolve modify/delete merge conflicts on files in the `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` list. Once `\"docs/\"` is added, any docs changes on `develop` that conflict with the stripped `release/alpha` branch will be handled automatically. This is correct behavior and requires no additional code.\n\nNote on the `startsWith` check: the conflict auto-resolution logic at `release.ts:380` checks `f.startsWith(s)` against entries in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`. Git reports file paths relative to repo root, e.g., `docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`. Since the strip entry is `\"docs/\"`, the `startsWith` match evaluates correctly: `\"docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx\".startsWith(\"docs/\")` is `true`. This has been verified against the code.\n\n**Step 3.4: Update `scripts/release.ts` schema handling for docs**\n\nAfter migration, `generate-schemas` writes schemas to both `schemas/` (runtime) and `docs/public/schemas/` (docs site). The release script needs three fixes to account for this:\n\n1. **Validation phase schema diff check** (line 427): The `git diff --name-only schemas/` check that warns about schema changes only covers `schemas/`. Extend it to also check `docs/public/schemas/`:\n\n```typescript\nconst schemaChanges = await exec(\"git diff --name-only schemas/ docs/public/schemas/\", {\n  silent: true,\n});\n```\n\nWithout this, the validation phase would silently miss changes to docs-side schemas. This is minor (both directories are generated from the same source), but including the check keeps the warning accurate.\n\n2. **Validation phase revert** (around line 436): The `git checkout` that reverts test schema generation currently only covers `schemas/`. Extend it to also revert `docs/public/schemas/`:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(\"git checkout -- schemas/ docs/public/schemas/ 2>/dev/null || true\", {\n  silent: true,\n  allowFailure: true,\n});\n```\n\nThe implementing agent should copy the exact structure of the existing line at line 436 and extend the path string only — do not refactor the error handling pattern. The existing code uses a belt-and-suspenders approach: shell-level `2>/dev/null || true` suppresses the error output and exit code, AND `allowFailure: true` prevents the `exec()` helper from throwing. Both are redundant with each other but both must be maintained because this is the existing pattern in the file. If `docs/public/schemas/` does not yet exist in the git index (e.g., schemas have never been generated and committed), `git checkout -- docs/public/schemas/` will fail, and both error suppression levels handle this gracefully.\n\nWithout this fix, the validation phase leaves `docs/public/schemas/` in a modified-but-unstaged state, which is messy and could cause the `git status --porcelain` check (at `release.ts:210`) in subsequent release runs to detect uncommitted changes.\n\n3. **Execute phase git add** (line 510): The release commit stages `package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/` but not `docs/public/schemas/`. After migration, schema generation writes to both directories, and the release commit should include both:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(\"git add package.json CHANGELOG.md schemas/ docs/public/schemas/\");\n```\n\nWithout this, the docs-side schemas would drift out of sync with the runtime schemas after each release — the runtime `schemas/` directory would be updated in the release commit, but `docs/public/schemas/` would retain the pre-release version until someone manually runs `generate-schemas` and commits.\n\n**Step 3.5: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` rsync exclude for `--use-head` mode**\n\nAt line 80, the `--use-head` rsync copies the entire repo working directory into a test snapshot. The actual code is a TypeScript template literal inside an `exec()` call:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(`rsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/`, { cwd: TEST_DIR });\n```\n\nAfter migration, this would copy the entire `docs/` workspace (including `docs/node_modules/`, `docs/.next/`, etc.) into the test snapshot. While the sync script's `prepare-snapshot` step will strip `docs/` later, copying it first is wasteful and makes the test significantly slower. Add `--exclude='docs'` to the rsync. The resulting line:\n\n```typescript\nawait exec(`rsync -av --exclude='.git' --exclude='node_modules' --exclude='intermediates' --exclude='docs' ${REPO_ROOT}/ private/`, { cwd: TEST_DIR });\n```\n\nThe implementing agent should edit this as a TypeScript `.ts` file (it's a template literal with `${REPO_ROOT}`), not as a shell script. This is consistent with the existing pattern: `intermediates/` is also excluded from the rsync even though it would be stripped by the sync script too.\n\n**Step 3.6: Update `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` `shouldNotExist`**\n\nAt line 160, add `\"docs\"` to the verification list:\n```typescript\nconst shouldNotExist = [\n  \"intermediates\",\n  \"external-docs\",\n  \"CLAUDE.md\",\n  \"Claude.md\",\n  \"CONTRIBUTING.md\",\n  \"PUBLISHING-PLAN.md\",\n  \"public-release-files\",\n  \".github/actions\",\n  \"scripts/sync-public.ts\",\n  \"scripts/test-sync-local.ts\",\n  \"scripts/release.ts\",\n  \"telemetry.md\",\n  \"standard-operating-procedures.md\",\n  \".github/workflows/ci.yml\",\n  \"docs\",\n];\n```\n\n### Phase 4: Update `.gitignore`\n\n**Step 4.1: Merge docs-specific ignores into root `.gitignore`**\n\nAdd the following to the root `.gitignore`:\n\n```gitignore\n# Docs build output\ndocs/.next/\ndocs/out/\n\n# Pagefind search index (generated at build time)\ndocs/public/_pagefind/\n```\n\nMost entries from the `hankweave-docs/.gitignore` (like `node_modules/`, `.env`, `.DS_Store`) are already covered by the root `.gitignore`. The root `.gitignore` already has generic `.next` and `out` entries (lines 141–142). In gitignore syntax, a pattern without a leading `/` or `/` in the middle matches anywhere in the directory tree. So `.next` matches both a root-level `.next/` and `docs/.next/` — this is tree-wide matching, not a coincidence. The `docs/`-prefixed additions proposed above (`docs/.next/`, `docs/out/`) are technically redundant with the existing unscoped patterns, but they make the intent explicit and are harmless.\n\nThe Pagefind `_pagefind/` pattern is docs-specific and is not currently in the root `.gitignore`. It should be added. The root `.gitignore` already has `dist` at line 147, which would match `docs/dist/` too via tree-wide matching. No additional entry needed for that.\n\nNote: the `hankweave-docs/.gitignore` lives at the outer wrapper level, not inside `nextra-site/`. It is NOT copied into `docs/` during the migration (see Step 1.1). The root `.gitignore` additions above are the only gitignore changes needed.\n\n### Phase 5: Update documentation\n\n**Step 5.1: Update `standard-operating-procedures.md` -- Repository Structure (Section 1)**\n\nUpdate the directory tree at lines 10-27 to include `docs/`:\n\n```\nhankweave-dev/              <- private repo (SouthBridgeAI/hankweave)\n├── server/                 <- runtime source code (TypeScript, Bun)\n├── scripts/                <- build, release, sync, and dev tooling\n├── tests/                  <- unit, integration, e2e, cross-runtime\n├── schemas/                <- generated JSON schemas (from Zod)\n├── shims/                  <- agent harness adapters\n├── docs/                   <- documentation site workspace (Next.js + Nextra)\n│   ├── app/                <- Next.js app directory\n│   ├── content/            <- MDX documentation pages\n│   ├── components/         <- custom React components\n│   ├── public/             <- static assets + generated schemas\n│   └── package.json        <- hankweave-docs workspace\n├── learning/               <- example hanks and guides (ships to public)\n├── intermediates/          <- planning docs, numbered by feature (internal only)\n├── external-docs/          <- SDK reference docs for agent context (internal only)\n├── public-release-files/   <- files overlaid onto the public repo during sync\n├── assets/                 <- images, demo GIFs\n├── .github/                <- CI workflows and composite actions\n├── CHANGELOG.md            <- Keep a Changelog format, [Unreleased] workflow\n├── CONTRIBUTING.md         <- branch model and PR guidelines (internal only)\n└── package.json            <- @southbridgeai/hankweave (private npm) + workspace root\n```\n\nAdd a note below the tree:\n\n```\nThe documentation site (`docs/`) deploys to Vercel from this repo. It is a Bun workspace\nbut does not have a workspace dependency on the runtime package.\n```\n\n**Step 5.2: Update \"What gets stripped\" section (lines 31-40)**\n\nAdd `docs/` to the directories list:\n\n```\n- **Directories**: `intermediates/`, `external-docs/`, `public-release-files/`, `.github/actions/`, `docs/`\n```\n\nImportant: the SOP's \"Directories\" list corresponds to `STRIP_DIRS` in `sync-public.ts`, while the \"Files\" list corresponds to `STRIP_FILES`. `docs` belongs in the directories list because it is added to `STRIP_DIRS`. The implementing agent should not accidentally remove or reorder existing entries (especially `CLAUDE.md`, `standard-operating-procedures.md`, etc., which are in the Files list and must remain).\n\nAlso add a note that `\"workspaces\"` is stripped from `package.json` during public sync transformation.\n\nAdditionally, update the prose sentence at SOP line 40 which describes the `package.json` transformation. It currently reads: \"The `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`, `homepage` points to the public repo, and `release:*` scripts are removed.\" This sentence should be updated to include the `workspaces` removal:\n\n```\nThe `package.json` is transformed: name becomes `hankweave`, `publishConfig.access` becomes `public`,\n`homepage` points to the public repo, `release:*` scripts are removed, and the `workspaces` field is\ndeleted (since docs are not included in the public repo).\n```\n\n**Step 5.3: Update Key Files Reference (Section 9, lines 337-354)**\n\nAdd docs workspace entries:\n\n```\n| `docs/package.json`         | Documentation site workspace (hankweave-docs)              |\n| `docs/content/`             | MDX documentation pages (concepts, guides, reference)      |\n```\n\n**Step 5.4: Add Documentation site note to Development Workflow (Section 3)**\n\nAdd a subsection:\n\n```markdown\n### Documentation site\n\nThe documentation site is a Bun workspace at `docs/`. To work on it locally,\nfirst ensure dependencies are installed from the repo root (`bun install`), then:\n\n    cd docs && bun run dev    # Development server with hot reload (uses --turbopack)\n    cd docs && bun run build  # Production build\n\nChanges to docs pages (`docs/content/**/*.mdx`) deploy automatically via Vercel when\npushed to the configured branch.\n\nSchema generation writes to both `schemas/` (for the npm package) and\n`docs/public/schemas/` (for the docs site):\n\n    bun run generate-schemas\n```\n\n### Phase 6: Verify everything works\n\n**Step 6.1: Run the full test suite**\n```bash\nbun run lint\nbun run tc\nbun run test:unit\nbun run test:integration\n```\n\nNone of these should be affected by the migration, provided the `tsconfig.json` exclude for `docs/**/*` was added in Step 2.1. Without that exclude, `bun run tc` will fail here. The runtime code is unchanged, test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/`, and Biome's `includes` whitelist only covers runtime files (not `docs/` or `scripts/`).\n\n**Step 6.2: Run schema generation**\n```bash\nbun run generate-schemas\n```\nVerify output exists in both `schemas/` and `docs/public/schemas/`.\n\n**Step 6.3: Run the runtime build**\n```bash\nbun run build\n```\nVerify `dist/index.js` is created. The build script uses `import.meta.dir` relative paths and `\"./server/index.ts\"` as entry point -- these are all unaffected by the workspace addition.\n\n**Step 6.4: Verify docs build**\n```bash\ncd docs && bun run build && cd ..\n```\nVerify Next.js build succeeds and the `postbuild` Pagefind step runs.\n\n**Step 6.5: Verify npm pack excludes docs**\n```bash\nnpm pack --dry-run\n```\nVerify the tarball file list contains only `dist/`, `shims/`, `schemas/`, and metadata files. The `docs/` directory should NOT appear because it's not in the `\"files\"` allowlist.\n\n**Step 6.6: Test the sync dry-run**\n```bash\nbun scripts/test-sync-local.ts --version <current-version> --use-head\n```\nVerify:\n- `docs/` is correctly stripped from the snapshot\n- `\"workspaces\"` is removed from the public `package.json`\n- All existing verification checks pass (the `shouldNotExist` and `shouldExist` lists)\n\n**Step 6.7: Test release dry-run (if on develop with clean tree)**\n```bash\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n```\nVerify validation passes. This exercises the merge feasibility check which now includes `\"docs/\"` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`.\n\n### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n\nThis is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change.\n\n**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n\n1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`.\n2. Under \"Git\" -> \"Connected Git Repository\", change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n3. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`.\n4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\".\n5. The `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json` specifies `\"framework\": \"nextjs\"`, `\"installCommand\": \"bun install\"`, `\"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"`, and contains a `\"build\": { \"env\": { \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\" } }` flag. This is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. It is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo, where it was a deliberate team decision. **This is a human-approval gate: the implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who owns the docs Vercel deployment and knows why this flag exists.** The agent cannot make this determination on their own.\n\n**Step 7.2: Install behavior with `docs/` as Root Directory**\n\nSetting Root Directory to `docs/` means Vercel picks up `docs/vercel.json` for its configuration (installCommand, buildCommand, CVE env flag). This is important: Vercel looks for `vercel.json` at the configured Root Directory. If Root Directory were set to `/` (repo root) instead, the `docs/vercel.json` would be ignored entirely — the installCommand, buildCommand, and the CVE override flag would not apply. That is why `docs/` must be the Root Directory.\n\nWith Root Directory set to `docs/`, Vercel checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `installCommand` (`\"bun install\"`) runs from `docs/`. Bun traverses upward from `docs/` to find the workspace root's `package.json` (which contains the `\"workspaces\"` field), then resolves and installs the full dependency tree from there. This is standard Bun workspace behavior — Bun always looks for the nearest ancestor `package.json` with a `\"workspaces\"` field.\n\n**If workspace resolution fails**: If `bun install` from `docs/` does not correctly find the workspace root (unlikely but not well-documented for Vercel's checkout structure), the fallback is to override `installCommand` in the Vercel dashboard to `\"cd .. && bun install\"`, which explicitly runs from the repo root. This is a dashboard-only change and does not require code modifications.\n\n**Alternative (repo-root approach)**: Set Root Directory to `/` and configure all commands via the Vercel dashboard (not `vercel.json`): `installCommand: \"bun install\"`, `buildCommand: \"cd docs && bun run build\"`. This guarantees workspace resolution but means `docs/vercel.json` is not used — all settings including the CVE env flag must be replicated in the Vercel dashboard. This approach is more fragile because dashboard settings are not version-controlled and the CVE flag could be silently lost.\n\n**Step 7.3: Configure \"Ignored Build Step\" (recommended)**\n\nTo prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n1. Go to Settings -> General -> \"Ignored Build Step\"\n2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n\nThis tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n\n**Caveat**: Vercel may perform shallow clones for deployments. If `HEAD^` is not available in a shallow clone, this command will fail. In that case, Vercel would proceed with the build (the command exits non-zero, which Vercel treats as \"do not skip\"). This is a safe failure mode — Vercel builds unnecessarily rather than skipping a needed build. If this becomes a problem (frequent unnecessary builds), the more robust alternative is to use Vercel's environment variable approach: a custom script that checks `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` against the previous deployment.\n\n**Step 7.4: Configure branch deployment**\n\nThe monorepo uses `develop` and `release/alpha` branches. Configure Vercel:\n- Production deployment: whichever branch the docs should deploy from (likely `develop` or `release/alpha`, depending on team preference)\n- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`\n\n### Phase 8: Commit, PR, and cleanup\n\n**Step 8.1: Commit the migration**\n\nCommit all changes on the feature branch. Ensure the regenerated `bun.lock` is staged — without it, CI's `bun install --frozen-lockfile` will fail on the first run after the PR is opened.\n\n```bash\ngit add bun.lock\n```\n\nCommit message:\n```\nMigrate to monorepo: bring docs into workspace\n\n- Add docs/ workspace (formerly SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs)\n- Add \"workspaces\" field to root package.json\n- Regenerate bun.lock as unified workspace lockfile\n- Exclude docs/**/* from root tsconfig.json\n- Update generate-schemas.ts to use monorepo-local docs path\n- Add docs/ to strip lists (sync-public.ts, release.ts, test-sync-local.ts)\n- Strip \"workspaces\" from public package.json in transformPackageJson()\n- Update release.ts schema handling for docs/public/schemas/\n- Add --exclude='docs' to test-sync-local.ts rsync\n- Update .gitignore for docs build artifacts\n- Update standard-operating-procedures.md\n```\n\nNote on merge convention: per SOP Section 4, the repo uses squash merges. When the PR is squash-merged into `develop`, only the PR title becomes the commit message on `develop`. The bullet-point list above is for the feature branch record and the PR body — it will not appear verbatim in `develop`'s git log.\n\n**Step 8.2: Open PR targeting develop**\n\nFollow the existing PR conventions per SOP Section 4:\n- Title: `Monorepo migration: consolidate hankweave-docs into workspace`\n- Body: Problem/approach/files-changed format\n\n**Step 8.3: Optional CHANGELOG entry**\n\nUnder `[Unreleased] > Changed`:\n```markdown\n- **Monorepo migration** -- Consolidated documentation site into the development repository as a Bun workspace. No changes to runtime behavior or public-facing interfaces.\n```\n\nThis is optional since it's an internal infrastructure change, not user-facing.\n\n**Step 8.4: Archive the old `hankweave-docs` repo (after migration is fully verified)**\n\nThis step happens last — after the PR is merged, CI passes on the merged branch, and the Vercel deployment from the monorepo is confirmed working. Do not archive the old repo before these are all green.\n\n1. Archive `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` on GitHub (set to read-only).\n2. Update the README to say \"This repo has been merged into SouthBridgeAI/hankweave.\"\n3. Disconnect Vercel from the old repo (after the new connection is verified).\n\n---\n\n## g. Testing Considerations\n\n### Existing tests affected\n\n**None.** No existing test file needs modification. The migration changes the root `package.json` (adds `\"workspaces\"`), `tsconfig.json` (adds `\"docs/**/*\"` to exclude), scripts in `scripts/` (path adjustments, strip list updates), `.gitignore`, and the SOP document. None of these are read by tests.\n\nSpecifically checked:\n- `tests/unit/json-schema.test.ts` reads from `schemas/` which is unchanged.\n- `tests/unit/config.test.ts` reads from `server/config.ts` which is unchanged.\n- `tests/unit/utils.test.ts` uses `getMetadata()` which reads version from `package.json` -- the `\"workspaces\"` field won't affect this.\n- All test imports use relative paths from `tests/` to `server/` which are unaffected.\n\n### New tests needed\n\nNo new automated tests are strictly required. The migration is a structural change, not a code change. Verification is manual (Phase 6 above).\n\nOptional: A lightweight monorepo smoke test could be added:\n```typescript\n// tests/unit/monorepo-structure.test.ts\nimport { describe, test, expect } from \"bun:test\";\nimport * as fs from \"node:fs\";\nimport * as path from \"node:path\";\n\ndescribe(\"monorepo structure\", () => {\n  const ROOT = path.resolve(import.meta.dir, \"../..\");\n\n  test(\"root package.json declares docs workspace\", () => {\n    const pkg = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT, \"package.json\"), \"utf8\"));\n    expect(pkg.workspaces).toContain(\"docs\");\n  });\n\n  test(\"docs workspace has package.json\", () => {\n    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"package.json\"))).toBe(true);\n  });\n\n  test(\"docs public directory exists\", () => {\n    expect(fs.existsSync(path.join(ROOT, \"docs\", \"public\"))).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nNote: An earlier version of this test checked `docs/public/schemas/` existence, but that directory is only created by `generate-schemas` and may not exist on a fresh checkout. The test now checks `docs/public/` instead, which is always present in the repo. If the team wants a schema-existence test, it should be in the schema generation test suite, not a structural smoke test.\n\nThis is a small structural invariant check. Only add if the team wants structural tests.\n\n### Edge cases to cover\n\n1. **`bun install --frozen-lockfile` in CI.** The new unified `bun.lock` must be committed. If it's missing, CI's `--frozen-lockfile` flag will fail. This is the composite action at `.github/actions/setup-environment/action.yml:34`.\n\n2. **npm publish from root.** The `\"files\"` field (`[\"dist\", \"shims\", \"schemas\"]`) is an allowlist. `docs/` is not listed, so it won't be in the npm tarball. Verify with `npm pack --dry-run`.\n\n3. **Dependency hoisting conflicts.** Bun workspaces hoists dependencies. The runtime and docs have almost entirely different dependency trees (server-side Bun tools vs. React/Next.js), so conflicts are unlikely. The only shared dev dependency is `typescript`, which can safely have independent versions per workspace.\n\n4. **Build executable.** `build-executable.ts` uses `ENTRY_POINT = \"server/index.ts\"` and builds from root. This path doesn't change with workspace addition.\n\n5. **Cross-runtime tests.** `run-cross-runtime-tests.ts` resolves `testsDir` from `import.meta.dirname`. This path doesn't change.\n\n6. **Cross-platform path separators.** The CI matrix includes Windows, macOS, and Linux. Workspace paths are resolved by Bun which handles path separators. No issues expected, but worth monitoring the first CI run after migration.\n\n7. **Root `tsconfig.json` include scope.** The `include: [\"**/*.ts\", \"**/*.tsx\"]` glob picks up everything in the repo tree. Without `\"docs/**/*\"` in `exclude`, `bun run tc` will fail on docs files (incompatible compiler options). This is addressed in Phase 2, Step 2.1, but listed here as an edge case because it is easy to miss in future workspace additions — any new workspace with TypeScript files will need the same exclude entry.\n\n8. **Biome scope does not cover scripts.** The `biome.json` `includes` list covers `server/**/*`, `tests/e2e/**/*`, `tests/utils/**/*`, `tests/unit/**/*`, and `types/**/*`. The `scripts/` directory is not included. This means the changes to `sync-public.ts`, `release.ts`, `test-sync-local.ts`, and `generate-schemas.ts` will not be linted by Biome. This is existing behavior, not a regression — just noting it so the implementing agent does not expect Biome to catch formatting issues in modified scripts.\n\n9. **`bunfig.toml` workspace scope.** The root `bunfig.toml` has only a `[test.env]` section setting `BUN_TEST = \"true\"`. This env var is consumed by `isNonInteractive()` in the runtime's `execution-setup.ts` to skip confirmation prompts during tests. It applies to `bun test` runs from the root, but all test scripts (`test:unit`, `test:integration`, etc.) are path-scoped to specific directories (`tests/unit`, `tests/integration`, etc.) and will not pick up docs files. No `bunfig.toml` changes are needed. The implementing agent should verify this remains true if the docs workspace ever adds its own test scripts.\n\n10. **`generate-schemas` becomes a hard failure if `docs/public/` is absent.** Before migration, if the sibling docs repo wasn't cloned, `docsAvailable` was `false` and the script silently skipped writing to docs. After migration, `docs/public/` is always present in a normal checkout, so `docsAvailable` is always `true`. If someone runs the script in an unusual environment where `docs/` is absent (sparse checkout, Docker build that mounts only `server/` and `schemas/`), the script will attempt to write to `docs/public/schemas/` and the filesystem write will fail — a hard error where it was previously a silent skip. This is acceptable for normal development but worth documenting. If the team runs `generate-schemas` in CI or Docker builds that don't check out the full repo, they should either ensure `docs/` is present or add explicit error handling.\n\n### CI enhancement (optional, recommended)\n\nConsider adding a docs build step to CI to prevent monorepo PRs from silently breaking docs:\n\n```yaml\n# In ci.yml, add a new job:\ndocs-build:\n  name: Docs Build Check\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  timeout-minutes: 10\n  steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n    - uses: ./.github/actions/setup-environment\n      with:\n        install-gemini: \"false\"\n    - name: Build docs\n      run: cd docs && bun run build\n```\n\nThis is a nice-to-have. Without it, a broken docs build would only be caught by Vercel deployment failures, not by the PR CI checks. Note that the docs build will run the `postbuild` pagefind step (`pagefind --site .next/server/app --output-path public/_pagefind`), which scans the full Next.js server output. The CI job may take longer than a typical build check because of this. The output goes to `docs/public/_pagefind/`, which is gitignored — it does not need to be persisted.\n\n---\n\n## h. Documentation Considerations\n\n### Docs to update\n\n1. **`standard-operating-procedures.md`** -- Repository structure diagram, strip list documentation, key files reference, development workflow section. Detailed in Phase 5 above.\n2. **This plan itself** -- Should be filed as an intermediate (e.g., `intermediates/45-monorepo-migration/`).\n\n### New docs needed\n\nNone required. The monorepo migration is an internal infrastructure change. The user-facing docs (`docs/content/`) document Hankweave as a CLI tool, not the development repo structure.\n\n### Docs that should NOT be updated\n\n- User-facing docs (`docs/content/guides/getting-started.mdx`, etc.) -- these reference `hankweave` as a CLI tool, not a repo layout.\n- The public `README.md` (in `public-release-files/`) -- describes the public `hankweave-runtime` repo.\n- The `CHANGELOG.md` -- unless the team wants the optional internal note described in Phase 8.\n\n---\n\n## i. Additional Resources\n\n### Bun workspaces\n\n- [bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces](https://bun.sh/docs/install/workspaces) -- primary reference\n- Root package can be a workspace member itself (important for the \"runtime at root\" approach)\n- `bun install` at root installs all workspace dependencies\n- `bun --filter <name> <script>` runs scripts in specific workspaces (useful for `bun --filter hankweave-docs build`)\n\n### Vercel monorepo support\n\n- Vercel's \"Root Directory\" setting specifies which subdirectory contains the deployable project\n- \"Ignored Build Step\" filters deploys to only relevant changes\n- Vercel handles Bun workspaces when it can see the full repo tree\n\n### Git subtree (optional history preservation)\n\n```bash\ngit remote add -f hankweave-docs <url>\ngit merge -s ours --no-commit --allow-unrelated-histories hankweave-docs/main\ngit read-tree --prefix=docs/ -u hankweave-docs/main\ngit commit -m \"Merge hankweave-docs into monorepo as docs workspace\"\n```\n\nThis preserves the full commit history of `hankweave-docs` at the `docs/` prefix. After this, restructure so the `nextra-site/` contents are at `docs/` root and remove unwanted wrapper files.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Changes\n\n| File | Change Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| `package.json` | Modify | Add `\"workspaces\": [\"docs\"]` (after `\"engines\"`) |\n| `tsconfig.json` | Modify | Add `\"docs/**/*\"` to `exclude` array |\n| `docs/` | Create (directory) | Contains flattened contents of `hankweave-docs/nextra-site/` |\n| `scripts/generate-schemas.ts` | Modify | Update `DOCS_SCHEMA_DIR` path (lines 24-32); update comment on `docsAvailable` (line 75) |\n| `scripts/sync-public.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs\"` to `STRIP_DIRS` (line 28); add `delete pkg.workspaces` to `transformPackageJson()` |\n| `scripts/release.ts` | Modify | Add `\"docs/\"` to `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP` (line 54); extend schema diff check to cover `docs/public/schemas/` (line 427); extend schema revert to cover `docs/public/schemas/` (line 436); extend `git add` to include `docs/public/schemas/` (line 510) |\n| `scripts/test-sync-local.ts` | Modify | Add `--exclude='docs'` to `--use-head` rsync (line 80); add `\"docs\"` to `shouldNotExist` (line 160) |\n| `.gitignore` | Modify | Add `docs/public/_pagefind/` and scoped `docs/.next/`, `docs/out/` patterns (technically redundant with existing unscoped patterns but explicit) |\n| `standard-operating-procedures.md` | Modify | Update repo structure, strip list, key files, package.json transform description (line 40), add docs section |\n| `bun.lock` | Regenerate | New unified lockfile covering both workspaces — must be staged and committed (Step 8.1) |\n\n**Total files modified**: 8 existing files + 1 new directory (~30 files from docs)\n**Total lines of code changed**: ~35 lines across scripts and configs + SOP update\n\nThe migration's success is measured by how little changes while still achieving the goal: a reliable, explicit relationship between runtime and docs in a single repo, with the public sync pipeline continuing to produce correct output.\n","numLines":891,"startLine":1,"totalLines":891}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:30:45.184Z"}
